{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788997594614,"sessionID":"ses_f776f37c6ffeLHNTo5P39JtFVe","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"toolu_01TobUM5v47LrjYZCxPPZbAo","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-10-round4/copilot-haiku45/PACKET.md"},"output":"<path>/Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-10-round4/copilot-haiku45/PACKET.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Audition packet — the exhaustiveness checker\n2: \n3: You are implementing one piece of a programming language toolchain FROM ITS\n4: SPECIFICATION ALONE. This is a clean-room exercise: a reference implementation\n5: exists, and you must not look at it.\n6: \n7: ## Hard rules\n8: \n9: - **Do not read, search for, or open any file outside this packet directory.**\n10:   A reference implementation of this language exists elsewhere on this machine,\n11:   as does a directory of design notes. Both are off limits. If you find\n12:   yourself looking for either, stop.\n13: - Do not modify anything under `cases/`.\n14: - Work only inside your own working directory.\n15: - Work autonomously. There is nobody to answer questions.\n16: \n17: ## What you are building\n18: \n19: A command-line program that decides whether a `switch` is well-formed and\n20: reports the problems it finds.\n21: \n22: Deliverable: an executable file named `switchcheck` in your working directory:\n23: \n24:     ./switchcheck <path-to-.bs-file>\n25: \n26: Any language available on the machine is fine, provided `./switchcheck` runs\n27: directly via a shebang. No network access, no package installs.\n28: \n29: ## Output contract\n30: \n31: For the file it is given, `switchcheck` prints **one lowercase tag per line** on\n32: stdout and nothing else — no prose, no filenames, no line numbers. A well-formed\n33: program prints **nothing at all**.\n34: \n35: These are the only tags you may print:\n36: \n37:     switch_inexhaustive\n38:     unreachable_arm\n39:     vacuous_arm\n40:     unsatisfiable_arm_guard\n41:     rebinding\n42:     unbound_variable\n43:     return_not_declared\n44:     arg_not_accepted\n45:     switch_in_guard\n46: \n47: **What each of those means is for you to determine from the specification\n48: below.** The names are listed so that your output can be compared\n49: mechanically — they are a vocabulary, not a definition of the rules.\n50: \n51: The specification is the language reference, not a task sheet, and it is given\n52: to you whole rather than filtered so that nothing it says is missing a reason.\n53: Where it describes a rule whose diagnostic is not in the list above, that rule\n54: is still true of the language — say nothing about it. Printing a tag outside the\n55: list fails a case exactly as a wrong tag would.\n56: \n57: Exit code is ignored. Order does not matter and duplicates are ignored; only\n58: the set of tags is compared.\n59: \n60: ## How you are marked\n61: \n62: Your `switchcheck` is run over the files in `cases/` and the tags it prints are\n63: compared against the tags the reference compiler produces for the same file.\n64: **Every case must match exactly.**\n65: \n66: Some of the files are well-formed and must produce no output at all. Printing a\n67: diagnostic for a correct program fails exactly as hard as missing one, so do not\n68: guess defensively: a checker that reports a problem whenever it is unsure fails\n69: on the first case. Test against `cases/` before you finish.\n70: \n71: ## The specification\n72: \n73: What follows is the relevant part of the language reference, verbatim. It is all\n74: you get, and it is meant to be enough. Where it is not enough, prefer the\n75: reading the text best supports rather than inventing a rule.\n76: \n77: ---\n78: \n79: ## 2. Multi-clause heads\n80: \n81: The one structural move the language rests on: C#'s pattern grammar moves out of `switch` arms and\n82: into the **parameter position**, and N declarations are allowed where C# allows one.\n83: \n84: <!-- check:\n85: type Verdict = :positive | :zero | :negative | :unknown\n86: -->\n87: ```csharp\n88: type Reading = (:ok, int) | (:error, atom)\n89: \n90: public Verdict Classify(Reading r)\n91: \n92: Classify((:ok, n)) when n > 0 -> :positive\n93: Classify((:ok, 0))            -> :zero\n94: Classify((:ok, n))            -> :negative\n95: Classify((:error, e))         -> :unknown\n96: ```\n97: \n98: Five clauses in, five native Erlang clause heads out. **shipped**\n99: \n100: **The signature is mandatory.** Exhaustiveness is only a well-posed question against a *declared*\n101: input type — a language that infers the function type from its own clauses can never ask it,\n102: because the answer is always yes. **shipped**\n103: \n104: **Guards** use `when`, with `and` and `or`. A guard the checker can read as a type operation\n105: refines the clause; one it cannot read credits nothing. **shipped**\n106: \n107: ```csharp\n108: Classify(n) when n < 10              -> :low\n109: Classify(n) when n >= 10 and n < 100 -> :mid\n110: Classify(n) when n >= 100            -> :high\n111: ```\n112: \n113: That is exhaustive over `int`, with no catch-all, because the checker carries real integer\n114: intervals.\n115: \n116: **One spelling, in every position** — guard, pattern and refinement predicate. There is no `&&` and\n117: no `||`; they were removed rather than kept as synonyms. This language puts patterns in the\n118: *parameter* position, so a pattern and a guard sit on the same line in every non-trivial function.\n119: C# separates its pattern `and` from its expression `&&` deliberately, and can afford to because\n120: patterns and expressions rarely touch there; here they always do.\n121: \n122: **There is no `not`, and no `!`.** Negation is not an operator in this language. The comparisons a\n123: guard admits already come in opposite pairs — `<=` against `>`, `>=` against `<`, `!=` against `==`\n124: — so the complement of any guard the checker can read is a guard you can already write, and a `not`\n125: would compile to exactly that. Where the checker *cannot* read a predicate, negating it buys nothing\n126: either: such a clause credits nothing towards exhaustiveness, and a refinement that cannot be\n127: translated is a hard error rather than a silent widening. Which case a clause takes is the head's\n128: job, not an operator's. Typing either spelling is met by a diagnostic naming the comparison to use\n129: instead. **shipped**\n130: \n131: A construct all four neighbouring languages have is refused here, so it is worth saying why it is\n132: not a divergence in practice. Every negation in a guard across OTP 28's `stdlib` and `kernel` — 16\n133: of them — wraps a type test or `is_map_key`. Type tests are absent from this language by design and\n134: `is_map_key` is a pattern, so the category those languages reach for `not` to negate is the category\n135: this one moved into the clause head.\n136: \n137: **A span of integers is a relational pattern.** `4..7` was refused: C#'s `..` builds a half-open\n138: slice over *indices*, is not enumerable, and in pattern position already means \"the rest\" — which\n139: this language uses for lists. **shipped**\n140: \n141: <!-- check:\n142: public atom Classify(int n)\n143: -->\n144: ```csharp\n145: Classify(>= 4 and <= 7) -> :reserved\n146: Classify(<= -1)         -> :negative\n147: Classify(>= 0 and <= 3) -> :low\n148: Classify(>= 8)          -> :high\n149: ```\n150: \n151: Those four clauses are **exhaustive over `int`** with no catch-all, which is the property worth\n152: looking at: a span is a set the checker subtracts, not a test it takes on trust. It goes where a\n153: whole argument goes — inside a record pattern, a tuple or a list, write the comparison as a guard.\n154: \n155: The rule this produced, which governs future borrowings: **borrow the construct, or don't borrow\n156: the glyph.** Where C# has the symbol but not the construct, taking the symbol buys no familiarity\n157: and costs a false friend.\n158: \n159: **A list pattern is a prefix, and a rest marker is optional.** `[a, b]` is exactly two; `[a, b, ..]`\n160: is two or more and discards the tail; `[a, b, ..t]` is two or more and binds it. The marker is a\n161: marker and not a pattern — `..` or `..name`, nothing else — so a list pattern says how long the list\n162: is and what is in the positions it names, and nothing about the rest. **shipped**\n163: \n164: `[a, b]` means exactly two in Erlang, Elixir, C# and Gleam alike. That is the one place this\n165: language's two reference families agree, so refusing it was the divergence rather than admitting it\n166: — and the refusal used to advise `[a, b, ..t]`, which means something else.\n167: \n168: <!-- check:\n169: public atom Dispatch(list<string> path)\n170: -->\n171: ```csharp\n172: Dispatch([\"orders\"])     -> :index\n173: Dispatch([\"orders\", id]) -> :show\n174: Dispatch(_)              -> :not_found\n175: ```\n176: \n177: That is how a route table distinguishes `/orders` from `/orders/42` without a length guard, and\n178: `/orders/42/lines` reaches the catch-all rather than being swallowed by the second clause.\n179: \n180: **The checker sees the length, and it does so without ever measuring one.** A non-empty list is a\n181: product of an element and a tail, subtracted by the same rule that already subtracts tuples exactly,\n182: so length falls out of the recursion rather than being carried beside it. The residual is then a\n183: clause you can paste: `[]` beside `[a, b, ..]` leaves `[int]` — exactly-one — where a language with\n184: an O(1) length would say `{ Length: 1 }` and this one has no `length` to say it with. Depth is\n185: bounded by the longest prefix any clause writes, per nesting level, which is what makes the\n186: recursion terminate. **shipped**\n187: \n188: A consequence worth stating: a closed residual over a list forbids a catch-all exactly as any other\n189: closed residual does, so a `list<bool>` missing its two length-one cases is an error naming\n190: `[true]` and `[false]` rather than a `_`. That bites only where the element type is closed — over\n191: `list<int>` the element is unbounded, the residual stays open, and `_` remains legal.\n192: \n193: **To match against a value a name already holds, write `== name`.** A bare name in a pattern\n194: introduces a name; `== name` matches the value that name is bound to. **shipped**\n195: \n196: So a head that repeats a bare name — `F(acc, acc)` — is an **error**, not an equality constraint:\n197: both are introductions, and the second rebinds what the first bound, which §1 forbids. `F(acc, ==\n198: acc)` is how you ask for the constraint. This is the whole reason the marker exists; without it the\n199: language has no way to say *the same value again*.\n200: \n201: ```csharp\n202: public int RunLength(int head, list<int> xs)\n203: \n204: RunLength(head, [])                -> 0\n205: RunLength(head, [== head, ..rest]) -> 1 + RunLength(head, rest)\n206: RunLength(head, [_, ..rest])       -> 0\n207: ```\n208: \n209: It is the **equality member of the relational family above**, so a reader who has met `>= 4` in a\n210: head reads `== acc` on sight. The family divides cleanly — **relational operators take a literal,\n211: `==` takes a name** — so `>= acc` is not a span bounded by a runtime value, and `== 4` is not a\n212: second spelling for the literal pattern `4`. Neither is admitted.\n213: \n214: The space is not significant: `==acc` and `== acc` are one program. Written with the space, to match\n215: `>= 4`.\n216: \n217: This is the one capability with no C# equivalent at all — C# patterns cannot match a runtime value,\n218: and push you to `when v == expected`. Here that workaround is worse than it looks, because it moves\n219: a pattern concern into a guard, and the checker reads `var == literal` but not `var == var`: the\n220: guard would credit nothing and the arm would subtract nothing from the residual. **A matched name\n221: credits nothing to the certain set either.** Its value is unknown at compile time, so it may narrow\n222: what is *possible* and never counts as coverage — a `switch` whose only non-catch-all arm matches a\n223: name is inexhaustive over the whole subject type.\n224: \n225: ---\n226: \n227: ---\n228: \n229: ## 3. Exhaustiveness\n230: \n231: **A function that does not cover its declared input does not compile.** No opt-out, no flag. The\n232: **residual is the missing case**, which is why the diagnostic below hands you a clause to paste\n233: rather than a complaint to interpret.\n234: \n235: ```csharp\n236: type Verdict = :positive | :zero | :negative | :unknown\n237: type Reading = (:ok, int) | (:error, atom)\n238: \n239: public Verdict Classify(Reading r)\n240: \n241: Classify((:ok, n)) when n > 0 -> :positive\n242: Classify((:ok, 0))            -> :zero\n243: Classify((:ok, n))            -> :negative\n244: Classify((:error, e))         -> :unknown\n245: ```\n246: \n247: Delete the third clause and the compiler answers:\n248: \n249: ```\n250: error: Classify is not exhaustive\n251:   no clause matches:\n252:     Classify((:ok, n)) when n <= -1 -> ...\n253: ```\n254: \n255: The error is the **missing clause**, not a complaint — the residual is computed exactly and printed\n256: as a head you can paste in. Where it is wide, the **printed** form stops after three cases and says\n257: how many it left; the residual itself is never summarised, and the full one is a query away.\n258: **shipped**, and the truncation **decided**\n259: \n260: A **catch-all is legal only where the residual is open** — over a `term`, or any type with an\n261: unbounded part. Where the compiler knows the remaining case names, `_` is an error: it would put\n262: the language's headline guarantee one character from being switched off invisibly. **shipped** —\n263: and the diagnostic names the discarded cases as a head to write instead.\n264: \n265: ---\n266: \n267: ---\n268: \n269: ## 5. Control flow\n270: \n271: **`switch` is the only branching construct.** There is no `if`, no `else`, no ternary.\n272: \n273: <!-- check:\n274: type Verdict = :new | :gone | :unknown\n275: record Order { Id: int, Status: atom }\n276: -->\n277: ```csharp\n278: public Verdict Describe(Order o)\n279: \n280: Describe(o) -> o.Status switch {\n281:     :placed  => :new,\n282:     :shipped => :gone,\n283:     _        => :unknown\n284: }\n285: ```\n286: \n287: The `_` here is legal because `Status` is an `atom` and the atom universe is open, so the residual\n288: cannot be enumerated — which is the only shape a catch-all is admitted over. Over a *closed*\n289: residual, where the compiler knows the missing case by name, §2 makes `_` an error telling you to\n290: name it — **shipped**, at a switch arm and at a clause head alike.\n291: \n292: For compound conditions, the subject is a **tuple** — which is the clause head's own shape, one\n293: level down:\n294: \n295: <!-- check:\n296: type Disposition = :ack | :dead_letter | :requeue\n297: -->\n298: ```csharp\n299: public Disposition Decide(bool ok, bool permanent, bool redelivered)\n300: \n301: Decide(o, p, r) -> (o, p, r) switch {\n302:     (true,  _,     _)     => :ack,\n303:     (false, true,  _)     => :dead_letter,\n304:     (false, false, false) => :requeue,\n305:     (false, false, true)  => :requeue\n306: }\n307: ```\n308: \n309: Exhaustive with **no catch-all**, and the compiler agrees. An arm takes the clause head's pattern\n310: grammar whole, so it may carry a guard, or be a relational pattern:\n311: \n312: ```csharp\n313: public atom Classify(int n)\n314: \n315: Classify(n) -> n switch {\n316:     m when m < 5 => :retried,\n317:     >= 5         => :exhausted\n318: }\n319: ```\n320: \n321: **The name a guarded arm introduces must be fresh** — `m` above, not `n`. §2's rule reaches here:\n322: a bare name in a pattern *introduces* a name, so an arm written `n when n < 5` where `n` is already\n323: the parameter is `rebinding`, not a match against it. **The guard is not what makes it an error.**\n324: A bare `n` as an arm pattern is rejected with no guard present at all; `m when m < 5` and a plain\n325: `< 5` are both accepted. To match the value `n` already holds, §2's spelling is `== n`.\n326: \n327: Nested inside a record pattern, `{ Deliveries: > 5 }` is not\n328: built: a relational pattern goes where a whole argument goes.\n329: \n330: **shipped** — F7.\n331: \n332: `else` is absent because it is what a *binary unnamed* conditional needs; every fall-through here is\n333: a pattern. `cond` is **open** — deliberately unpaid-for until the shape is shown to occur. Measured\n334: so far: a four-wide tuple reads fine.\n335: \n336: ### What the compiler says about a switch\n337: \n338: A `switch` is **checked**, not merely compiled, and there are nine things it can be told. Every\n339: one names the file, the line and the enclosing function, and hands back the material needed to fix\n340: it rather than only reporting that something was wrong.\n341: \n342: Five of these are about the arms as a set, three are about what an arm's body does, and one is\n343: about where a `switch` may appear at all.\n344: \n345: **A switch must cover its subject.** If some value of the subject's type matches no arm, that is\n346: `switch_inexhaustive`, and the message hands back the arm you have not written:\n347: \n348: ```csharp\n349: public atom Ready(bool b)\n350: \n351: Ready(b) -> b switch {\n352:     true => :yes\n353: }\n354: ```\n355: \n356: — *this switch in `Ready` is not exhaustive; no arm matches: `false => ...`*. The residual is the\n357: missing case, so what makes the error legitimate is the same thing that answers it.\n358: \n359: **An arm every earlier arm already covers is dead.** That is `unreachable_arm`, and it is a\n360: **warning** rather than an error: the program still compiles, because the arm changes nothing.\n361: The message counts arms from one:\n362: \n363: ```csharp\n364: public atom Which(atom a)\n365: \n366: Which(a) -> a switch {\n367:     _  => :any,\n368:     :x => :ex\n369: }\n370: ```\n371: \n372: — *arm 2 of this switch in `Which` is unreachable; every value it matches is matched by an earlier\n373: arm.* Note that the catch-all is legal here, by the rule at the top of this section: `a` is an\n374: `atom`, so the residual is open.\n375: \n376: **An arm may also be dead without any earlier arm covering it**, and the two are not the same\n377: mistake. If the arm's pattern is not a member of the subject's type at all, no value can reach it\n378: no matter what the other arms do — so it is `vacuous_arm`, and the message names the type rather\n379: than sending you to look for an earlier arm there is no reason to expect:\n380: \n381: ```csharp\n382: type Verdict = :pass | :fail\n383: \n384: public int Score(Verdict v)\n385: \n386: Score(v) -> v switch {\n387:     (:pass, n) => n,\n388:     :pass      => 1,\n389:     :fail      => 0\n390: }\n391: ```\n392: \n393: — *arm 1 of this switch in `Score` matches no value; the subject's type is `:pass | :fail`, and\n394: this arm's pattern is not a member of it.* This is the shape the tagged unions of other languages\n395: teach you to write. A `Verdict` is `:pass | :fail`, so `(:pass, n)` is a two-tuple that no\n396: `Verdict` ever is — and the same reading error is what makes `(:some, s)` the first thing most\n397: people try on an `option<T>`, which is `T | :nothing` and **untagged**. The type is the half you\n398: do not have when you make it, so the message is the half that hands it over.\n399: \n400: **And an arm can be dead because of its guard rather than its pattern.** Then the pattern is a\n401: perfectly good member of the subject's type and the guard admits nothing, which is\n402: `unsatisfiable_arm_guard` — a third repair, and a message that deliberately does *not* name the\n403: type, because the type is not what is wrong:\n404: \n405: ```csharp\n406: public int Grade(int n)\n407: \n408: Grade(n) -> n switch {\n409:     x when x > 5 and x < 3 => 0,\n410:     x                      => 1\n411: }\n412: ```\n413: \n414: — *arm 1 of this switch in `Grade` has an unsatisfiable guard; the pattern is a member of the\n415: subject's type, it is the guard that admits nothing.* A guard the compiler **cannot read** — one\n416: comparing two variables, say — is not this, and is never reported: an arm is judged on its pattern\n417: alone there, rather than the compiler announcing its own ignorance as your mistake.\n418: \n419: **A name in an arm pattern is introduced, never matched against.** An arm whose pattern is a bare\n420: name already in scope is `rebinding` — the rule §2 states for clause heads, reaching arms\n421: unchanged, and the paragraph above gives it in full:\n422: \n423: ```csharp\n424: public atom Pick(int n, term e)\n425: \n426: Pick(n, e) -> e switch {\n427:     n => :same,\n428:     _ => :other\n429: }\n430: ```\n431: \n432: — *`Pick` binds `n` twice; a name means one thing in a clause. There is no mutation to assign\n433: with, so rename the second one.* Renaming is one way out; the paragraph above gives the other, for\n434: when you meant to match the value `n` already holds rather than introduce a new name: `== n`.\n435: \n436: **An arm's bindings are its own.** A name bound by one arm's pattern is not in scope in another\n437: arm's body; each arm is a separate branch, and only one of them runs. Reaching for a neighbour's\n438: name is `unbound_variable`:\n439: \n440: ```csharp\n441: public term Bad(term e)\n442: \n443: Bad(e) -> e switch {\n444:     (:ok, v) => w,\n445:     (:no, w) => w\n446: }\n447: ```\n448: \n449: — *`Bad` uses `w`, which nothing binds; a name comes from a clause head or a binding above it.*\n450: The second arm is well-formed: `w` is bound by its own pattern and used in its own body.\n451: \n452: **Every arm returns a value the signature declares.** The declared return type covers the whole\n453: `switch`, not each arm separately, so a single arm returning something outside it is\n454: `return_not_declared`:\n455: \n456: ```csharp\n457: public atom Verdict(bool b)\n458: \n459: Verdict(b) -> b switch {\n460:     true  => :yes,\n461:     false => 0\n462: }\n463: ```\n464: \n465: — *`Verdict` returns a value its signature does not declare; not covered by the declared return\n466: type: `0`.* Where the clauses justify a wider signature, the message also offers the one they\n467: support, so the fix can be to the declaration rather than to the body.\n468: \n469: **An arm's body is checked against what it calls.** A value that reaches an arm still has to\n470: satisfy the functions that arm hands it to; if it does not, that is `arg_not_accepted`, reported\n471: against the *caller*:\n472: \n473: ```csharp\n474: public bool Big(int n)\n475: Big(n) -> n > 100\n476: \n477: public atom Tag(atom a)\n478: Tag(a) -> :seen\n479: \n480: public atom Check(int n)\n481: Check(n) -> n switch {\n482:     m when Big(m) => Tag(m),\n483:     _             => :small\n484: }\n485: ```\n486: \n487: — *`Check` hands `Tag` an argument it does not accept; argument 1 is not covered by `Tag`'s\n488: declared type: `int`.* The proposed edit is always to the function being checked, never to the\n489: callee: the fix is `Check`'s to make.\n490: \n491: **A guard may not branch.** A guard asks a question about values a clause has already matched, so a\n492: `switch` inside one is `switch_in_guard` — a parse the expression grammar allows and the checker\n493: refuses:\n494: \n495: ```csharp\n496: public atom F(atom x)\n497: \n498: F(x) when x switch { :a => true, _ => false } -> :yes\n499: F(x) -> :no\n500: ```\n501: \n502: — *`F` has a switch in a guard; a guard asks a question about the values a clause already matched,\n503: it cannot branch. Move the switch into the body.*\n504: \n505: \n506: ---\n\n(End of file - total 506 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Audition packet — the exhaustiveness checker\n\nYou are implementing one piece of a programming language toolchain FROM ITS\nSPECIFICATION ALONE. This is a clean-room exercise: a reference implementation\nexists, and you must not look at it.\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- **Do not read, search for, or open any file outside this packet directory.**\n  A reference implementation of this language exists elsewhere on this machine,\n  as does a directory of design notes. Both are off limits. If you find\n  yourself looking for either, stop.\n- Do not modify anything under `cases/`.\n- Work only inside your own working directory.\n- Work autonomously. There is nobody to answer questions.\n\n## What you are building\n\nA command-line program that decides whether a `switch` is well-formed and\nreports the problems it finds.","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-10-round4/copilot-haiku45/PACKET.md","text":"# Audition packet — the exhaustiveness checker\n\nYou are implementing one piece of a programming language toolchain FROM ITS\nSPECIFICATION ALONE. This is a clean-room exercise: a reference implementation\nexists, and you must not look at it.\n\n## Hard rules\n\n- **Do not read, search for, or open any file outside this packet directory.**\n  A reference implementation of this language exists elsewhere on this machine,\n  as does a directory of design notes. Both are off limits. If you find\n  yourself looking for either, stop.\n- Do not modify anything under `cases/`.\n- Work only inside your own working directory.\n- Work autonomously. There is nobody to answer questions.\n\n## What you are building\n\nA command-line program that decides whether a `switch` is well-formed and\nreports the problems it finds.\n\nDeliverable: an executable file named `switchcheck` in your working directory:\n\n    ./switchcheck <path-to-.bs-file>\n\nAny language available on the machine is fine, provided `./switchcheck` runs\ndirectly via a shebang. No network access, no package installs.\n\n## Output contract\n\nFor the file it is given, `switchcheck` prints **one lowercase tag per line** on\nstdout and nothing else — no prose, no filenames, no line numbers. A well-formed\nprogram prints **nothing at all**.\n\nThese are the only tags you may print:\n\n    switch_inexhaustive\n    unreachable_arm\n    vacuous_arm\n    unsatisfiable_arm_guard\n    rebinding\n    unbound_variable\n    return_not_declared\n    arg_not_accepted\n    switch_in_guard\n\n**What each of those means is for you to determine from the specification\nbelow.** The names are listed so that your output can be compared\nmechanically — they are a vocabulary, not a definition of the rules.\n\nThe specification is the language reference, not a task sheet, and it is given\nto you whole rather than filtered so that nothing it says is missing a reason.\nWhere it describes a rule whose diagnostic is not in the list above, that rule\nis still true of the language — say nothing about it. Printing a tag outside the\nlist fails a case exactly as a wrong tag would.\n\nExit code is ignored. Order does not matter and duplicates are ignored; only\nthe set of tags is compared.\n\n## How you are marked\n\nYour `switchcheck` is run over the files in `cases/` and the tags it prints are\ncompared against the tags the reference compiler produces for the same file.\n**Every case must match exactly.**\n\nSome of the files are well-formed and must produce no output at all. Printing a\ndiagnostic for a correct program fails exactly as hard as missing one, so do not\nguess defensively: a checker that reports a problem whenever it is unsure fails\non the first case. Test against `cases/` before you finish.\n\n## The specification\n\nWhat follows is the relevant part of the language reference, verbatim. It is all\nyou get, and it is meant to be enough. Where it is not enough, prefer the\nreading the text best supports rather than inventing a rule.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Multi-clause heads\n\nThe one structural move the language rests on: C#'s pattern grammar moves out of `switch` arms and\ninto the **parameter position**, and N declarations are allowed where C# allows one.\n\n<!-- check:\ntype Verdict = :positive | :zero | :negative | :unknown\n-->\n```csharp\ntype Reading = (:ok, int) | (:error, atom)\n\npublic Verdict Classify(Reading r)\n\nClassify((:ok, n)) when n > 0 -> :positive\nClassify((:ok, 0))            -> :zero\nClassify((:ok, n))            -> :negative\nClassify((:error, e))         -> :unknown\n```\n\nFive clauses in, five native Erlang clause heads out. **shipped**\n\n**The signature is mandatory.** Exhaustiveness is only a well-posed question against a *declared*\ninput type — a language that infers the function type from its own clauses can never ask it,\nbecause the answer is always yes. **shipped**\n\n**Guards** use `when`, with `and` and `or`. A guard the checker can read as a type operation\nrefines the clause; one it cannot read credits nothing. **shipped**\n\n```csharp\nClassify(n) when n < 10              -> :low\nClassify(n) when n >= 10 and n < 100 -> :mid\nClassify(n) when n >= 100            -> :high\n```\n\nThat is exhaustive over `int`, with no catch-all, because the checker carries real integer\nintervals.\n\n**One spelling, in every position** — guard, pattern and refinement predicate. There is no `&&` and\nno `||`; they were removed rather than kept as synonyms. This language puts patterns in the\n*parameter* position, so a pattern and a guard sit on the same line in every non-trivial function.\nC# separates its pattern `and` from its expression `&&` deliberately, and can afford to because\npatterns and expressions rarely touch there; here they always do.\n\n**There is no `not`, and no `!`.** Negation is not an operator in this language. The comparisons a\nguard admits already come in opposite pairs — `<=` against `>`, `>=` against `<`, `!=` against `==`\n— so the complement of any guard the checker can read is a guard you can already write, and a `not`\nwould compile to exactly that. Where the checker *cannot* read a predicate, negating it buys nothing\neither: such a clause credits nothing towards exhaustiveness, and a refinement that cannot be\ntranslated is a hard error rather than a silent widening. Which case a clause takes is the head's\njob, not an operator's. Typing either spelling is met by a diagnostic naming the comparison to use\ninstead. **shipped**\n\nA construct all four neighbouring languages have is refused here, so it is worth saying why it is\nnot a divergence in practice. Every negation in a guard across OTP 28's `stdlib` and `kernel` — 16\nof them — wraps a type test or `is_map_key`. Type tests are absent from this language by design and\n`is_map_key` is a pattern, so the category those languages reach for `not` to negate is the category\nthis one moved into the clause head.\n\n**A span of integers is a relational pattern.** `4..7` was refused: C#'s `..` builds a half-open\nslice over *indices*, is not enumerable, and in pattern position already means \"the rest\" — which\nthis language uses for lists. **shipped**\n\n<!-- check:\npublic atom Classify(int n)\n-->\n```csharp\nClassify(>= 4 and <= 7) -> :reserved\nClassify(<= -1)         -> :negative\nClassify(>= 0 and <= 3) -> :low\nClassify(>= 8)          -> :high\n```\n\nThose four clauses are **exhaustive over `int`** with no catch-all, which is the property worth\nlooking at: a span is a set the checker subtracts, not a test it takes on trust. It goes where a\nwhole argument goes — inside a record pattern, a tuple or a list, write the comparison as a guard.\n\nThe rule this produced, which governs future borrowings: **borrow the construct, or don't borrow\nthe glyph.** Where C# has the symbol but not the construct, taking the symbol buys no familiarity\nand costs a false friend.\n\n**A list pattern is a prefix, and a rest marker is optional.** `[a, b]` is exactly two; `[a, b, ..]`\nis two or more and discards the tail; `[a, b, ..t]` is two or more and binds it. The marker is a\nmarker and not a pattern — `..` or `..name`, nothing else — so a list pattern says how long the list\nis and what is in the positions it names, and nothing about the rest. **shipped**\n\n`[a, b]` means exactly two in Erlang, Elixir, C# and Gleam alike. That is the one place this\nlanguage's two reference families agree, so refusing it was the divergence rather than admitting it\n— and the refusal used to advise `[a, b, ..t]`, which means something else.\n\n<!-- check:\npublic atom Dispatch(list<string> path)\n-->\n```csharp\nDispatch([\"orders\"])     -> :index\nDispatch([\"orders\", id]) -> :show\nDispatch(_)              -> :not_found\n```\n\nThat is how a route table distinguishes `/orders` from `/orders/42` without a length guard, and\n`/orders/42/lines` reaches the catch-all rather than being swallowed by the second clause.\n\n**The checker sees the length, and it does so without ever measuring one.** A non-empty list is a\nproduct of an element and a tail, subtracted by the same rule that already subtracts tuples exactly,\nso length falls out of the recursion rather than being carried beside it. The residual is then a\nclause you can paste: `[]` beside `[a, b, ..]` leaves `[int]` — exactly-one — where a language with\nan O(1) length would say `{ Length: 1 }` and this one has no `length` to say it with. Depth is\nbounded by the longest prefix any clause writes, per nesting level, which is what makes the\nrecursion terminate. **shipped**\n\nA consequence worth stating: a closed residual over a list forbids a catch-all exactly as any other\nclosed residual does, so a `list<bool>` missing its two length-one cases is an error naming\n`[true]` and `[false]` rather than a `_`. That bites only where the element type is closed — over\n`list<int>` the element is unbounded, the residual stays open, and `_` remains legal.\n\n**To match against a value a name already holds, write `== name`.** A bare name in a pattern\nintroduces a name; `== name` matches the value that name is bound to. **shipped**\n\nSo a head that repeats a bare name — `F(acc, acc)` — is an **error**, not an equality constraint:\nboth are introductions, and the second rebinds what the first bound, which §1 forbids. `F(acc, ==\nacc)` is how you ask for the constraint. This is the whole reason the marker exists; without it the\nlanguage has no way to say *the same value again*.\n\n```csharp\npublic int RunLength(int head, list<int> xs)\n\nRunLength(head, [])                -> 0\nRunLength(head, [== head, ..rest]) -> 1 + RunLength(head, rest)\nRunLength(head, [_, ..rest])       -> 0\n```\n\nIt is the **equality member of the relational family above**, so a reader who has met `>= 4` in a\nhead reads `== acc` on sight. The family divides cleanly — **relational operators take a literal,\n`==` takes a name** — so `>= acc` is not a span bounded by a runtime value, and `== 4` is not a\nsecond spelling for the literal pattern `4`. Neither is admitted.\n\nThe space is not significant: `==acc` and `== acc` are one program. Written with the space, to match\n`>= 4`.\n\nThis is the one capability with no C# equivalent at all — C# patterns cannot match a runtime value,\nand push you to `when v == expected`. Here that workaround is worse than it looks, because it moves\na pattern concern into a guard, and the checker reads `var == literal` but not `var == var`: the\nguard would credit nothing and the arm would subtract nothing from the residual. **A matched name\ncredits nothing to the certain set either.** Its value is unknown at compile time, so it may narrow\nwhat is *possible* and never counts as coverage — a `switch` whose only non-catch-all arm matches a\nname is inexhaustive over the whole subject type.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 3. Exhaustiveness\n\n**A function that does not cover its declared input does not compile.** No opt-out, no flag. The\n**residual is the missing case**, which is why the diagnostic below hands you a clause to paste\nrather than a complaint to interpret.\n\n```csharp\ntype Verdict = :positive | :zero | :negative | :unknown\ntype Reading = (:ok, int) | (:error, atom)\n\npublic Verdict Classify(Reading r)\n\nClassify((:ok, n)) when n > 0 -> :positive\nClassify((:ok, 0))            -> :zero\nClassify((:ok, n))            -> :negative\nClassify((:error, e))         -> :unknown\n```\n\nDelete the third clause and the compiler answers:\n\n```\nerror: Classify is not exhaustive\n  no clause matches:\n    Classify((:ok, n)) when n <= -1 -> ...\n```\n\nThe error is the **missing clause**, not a complaint — the residual is computed exactly and printed\nas a head you can paste in. Where it is wide, the **printed** form stops after three cases and says\nhow many it left; the residual itself is never summarised, and the full one is a query away.\n**shipped**, and the truncation **decided**\n\nA **catch-all is legal only where the residual is open** — over a `term`, or any type with an\nunbounded part. Where the compiler knows the remaining case names, `_` is an error: it would put\nthe language's headline guarantee one character from being switched off invisibly. **shipped** —\nand the diagnostic names the discarded cases as a head to write instead.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 5. Control flow\n\n**`switch` is the only branching construct.** There is no `if`, no `else`, no ternary.\n\n<!-- check:\ntype Verdict = :new | :gone | :unknown\nrecord Order { Id: int, Status: atom }\n-->\n```csharp\npublic Verdict Describe(Order o)\n\nDescribe(o) -> o.Status switch {\n    :placed  => :new,\n    :shipped => :gone,\n    _        => :unknown\n}\n```\n\nThe `_` here is legal because `Status` is an `atom` and the atom universe is open, so the residual\ncannot be enumerated — which is the only shape a catch-all is admitted over. Over a *closed*\nresidual, where the compiler knows the missing case by name, §2 makes `_` an error telling you to\nname it — **shipped**, at a switch arm and at a clause head alike.\n\nFor compound conditions, the subject is a **tuple** — which is the clause head's own shape, one\nlevel down:\n\n<!-- check:\ntype Disposition = :ack | :dead_letter | :requeue\n-->\n```csharp\npublic Disposition Decide(bool ok, bool permanent, bool redelivered)\n\nDecide(o, p, r) -> (o, p, r) switch {\n    (true,  _,     _)     => :ack,\n    (false, true,  _)     => :dead_letter,\n    (false, false, false) => :requeue,\n    (false, false, true)  => :requeue\n}\n```\n\nExhaustive with **no catch-all**, and the compiler agrees. An arm takes the clause head's pattern\ngrammar whole, so it may carry a guard, or be a relational pattern:\n\n```csharp\npublic atom Classify(int n)\n\nClassify(n) -> n switch {\n    m when m < 5 => :retried,\n    >= 5         => :exhausted\n}\n```\n\n**The name a guarded arm introduces must be fresh** — `m` above, not `n`. §2's rule reaches here:\na bare name in a pattern *introduces* a name, so an arm written `n when n < 5` where `n` is already\nthe parameter is `rebinding`, not a match against it. **The guard is not what makes it an error.**\nA bare `n` as an arm pattern is rejected with no guard present at all; `m when m < 5` and a plain\n`< 5` are both accepted. To match the value `n` already holds, §2's spelling is `== n`.\n\nNested inside a record pattern, `{ Deliveries: > 5 }` is not\nbuilt: a relational pattern goes where a whole argument goes.\n\n**shipped** — F7.\n\n`else` is absent because it is what a *binary unnamed* conditional needs; every fall-through here is\na pattern. `cond` is **open** — deliberately unpaid-for until the shape is shown to occur. Measured\nso far: a four-wide tuple reads fine.\n\n### What the compiler says about a switch\n\nA `switch` is **checked**, not merely compiled, and there are nine things it can be told. Every\none names the file, the line and the enclosing function, and hands back the material needed to fix\nit rather than only reporting that something was wrong.\n\nFive of these are about the arms as a set, three are about what an arm's body does, and one is\nabout where a `switch` may appear at all.\n\n**A switch must cover its subject.** If some value of the subject's type matches no arm, that is\n`switch_inexhaustive`, and the message hands back the arm you have not written:\n\n```csharp\npublic atom Ready(bool b)\n\nReady(b) -> b switch {\n    true => :yes\n}\n```\n\n— *this switch in `Ready` is not exhaustive; no arm matches: `false => ...`*. The residual is the\nmissing case, so what makes the error legitimate is the same thing that answers it.\n\n**An arm every earlier arm already covers is dead.** That is `unreachable_arm`, and it is a\n**warning** rather than an error: the program still compiles, because the arm changes nothing.\nThe message counts arms from one:\n\n```csharp\npublic atom Which(atom a)\n\nWhich(a) -> a switch {\n    _  => :any,\n    :x => :ex\n}\n```\n\n— *arm 2 of this switch in `Which` is unreachable; every value it matches is matched by an earlier\narm.* Note that the catch-all is legal here, by the rule at the top of this section: `a` is an\n`atom`, so the residual is open.\n\n**An arm may also be dead without any earlier arm covering it**, and the two are not the same\nmistake. If the arm's pattern is not a member of the subject's type at all, no value can reach it\nno matter what the other arms do — so it is `vacuous_arm`, and the message names the type rather\nthan sending you to look for an earlier arm there is no reason to expect:\n\n```csharp\ntype Verdict = :pass | :fail\n\npublic int Score(Verdict v)\n\nScore(v) -> v switch {\n    (:pass, n) => n,\n    :pass      => 1,\n    :fail      => 0\n}\n```\n\n— *arm 1 of this switch in `Score` matches no value; the subject's type is `:pass | :fail`, and\nthis arm's pattern is not a member of it.* This is the shape the tagged unions of other languages\nteach you to write. A `Verdict` is `:pass | :fail`, so `(:pass, n)` is a two-tuple that no\n`Verdict` ever is — and the same reading error is what makes `(:some, s)` the first thing most\npeople try on an `option<T>`, which is `T | :nothing` and **untagged**. The type is the half you\ndo not have when you make it, so the message is the half that hands it over.\n\n**And an arm can be dead because of its guard rather than its pattern.** Then the pattern is a\nperfectly good member of the subject's type and the guard admits nothing, which is\n`unsatisfiable_arm_guard` — a third repair, and a message that deliberately does *not* name the\ntype, because the type is not what is wrong:\n\n```csharp\npublic int Grade(int n)\n\nGrade(n) -> n switch {\n    x when x > 5 and x < 3 => 0,\n    x                      => 1\n}\n```\n\n— *arm 1 of this switch in `Grade` has an unsatisfiable guard; the pattern is a member of the\nsubject's type, it is the guard that admits nothing.* A guard the compiler **cannot read** — one\ncomparing two variables, say — is not this, and is never reported: an arm is judged on its pattern\nalone there, rather than the compiler announcing its own ignorance as your mistake.\n\n**A name in an arm pattern is introduced, never matched against.** An arm whose pattern is a bare\nname already in scope is `rebinding` — the rule §2 states for clause heads, reaching arms\nunchanged, and the paragraph above gives it in full:\n\n```csharp\npublic atom Pick(int n, term e)\n\nPick(n, e) -> e switch {\n    n => :same,\n    _ => :other\n}\n```\n\n— *`Pick` binds `n` twice; a name means one thing in a clause. There is no mutation to assign\nwith, so rename the second one.* Renaming is one way out; the paragraph above gives the other, for\nwhen you meant to match the value `n` already holds rather than introduce a new name: `== n`.\n\n**An arm's bindings are its own.** A name bound by one arm's pattern is not in scope in another\narm's body; each arm is a separate branch, and only one of them runs. Reaching for a neighbour's\nname is `unbound_variable`:\n\n```csharp\npublic term Bad(term e)\n\nBad(e) -> e switch {\n    (:ok, v) => w,\n    (:no, w) => w\n}\n```\n\n— *`Bad` uses `w`, which nothing binds; a name comes from a clause head or a binding above it.*\nThe second arm is well-formed: `w` is bound by its own pattern and used in its own body.\n\n**Every arm returns a value the signature declares.** The declared return type covers the whole\n`switch`, not each arm separately, so a single arm returning something outside it is\n`return_not_declared`:\n\n```csharp\npublic atom Verdict(bool b)\n\nVerdict(b) -> b switch {\n    true  => :yes,\n    false => 0\n}\n```\n\n— *`Verdict` returns a value its signature does not declare; not covered by the declared return\ntype: `0`.* Where the clauses justify a wider signature, the message also offers the one they\nsupport, so the fix can be to the declaration rather than to the body.\n\n**An arm's body is checked against what it calls.** A value that reaches an arm still has to\nsatisfy the functions that arm hands it to; if it does not, that is `arg_not_accepted`, reported\nagainst the *caller*:\n\n```csharp\npublic bool Big(int n)\nBig(n) -> n > 100\n\npublic atom Tag(atom a)\nTag(a) -> :seen\n\npublic atom Check(int n)\nCheck(n) -> n switch {\n    m when Big(m) => Tag(m),\n    _             => :small\n}\n```\n\n— *`Check` hands `Tag` an argument it does not accept; argument 1 is not covered by `Tag`'s\ndeclared type: `int`.* The proposed edit is always to the function being checked, never to the\ncallee: the fix is `Check`'s to make.\n\n**A guard may not branch.** A guard asks a question about values a clause has already matched, so a\n`switch` inside one is `switch_in_guard` — a parse the expression grammar allows and the checker\nrefuses:\n\n```csharp\npublic atom F(atom x)\n\nF(x) when x switch { :a => true, _ => false } -> :yes\nF(x) -> :no\n```\n\n— *`F` has a switch in a guard; a guard asks a question about the values a clause already matched,\nit cannot branch. Move the switch into the body.*\n\n\n---","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":506,"totalLines":506,"truncated":false}},"title":"Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-10-round4/copilot-haiku45/PACKET.md","time":{"start":1788997594556,"end":1788997594584}},"metadata":{"anthropic":{"caller":{"type":"direct"}}},"id":"prt_08890ed97001Sb3G8C70aF6fkm","sessionID":"ses_f776f37c6ffeLHNTo5P39JtFVe","messageID":"msg_08890e64f001FZkERm7zB39fce"}}
