{"type":"text","timestamp":1788997805694,"sessionID":"ses_f776f37c6ffeLHNTo5P39JtFVe","part":{"id":"prt_088940b6f0013jU01m5riZ2G5I","messageID":"msg_08894070d001dgWuDuFcPKGWzu","sessionID":"ses_f776f37c6ffeLHNTo5P39JtFVe","type":"text","text":"Ah! In c07, the arm pattern is `n when n < 5`. My code extracts the bare name `n` from the pattern, and since `n` is also a parameter, it reports `rebinding`. But that's wrong - the guard makes it a match on the parameter, not a rebinding.\n\nReading the spec more carefully: \"an arm whose pattern is a bare name already in scope is `rebinding`\". The pattern here is `n`, which is a bare name in scope. But wait, let me reread the spec section on this.\n\nFrom the spec: \"A name in an arm pattern is introduced, never matched against. An arm whose pattern is a bare name already in scope is rebinding\".\n\nBut also: \"The name a guarded arm introduces must be fresh — m above, not n.\"\n\nAnd: \"A bare n as an arm pattern is rejected with no guard present at all; m when m < 5 and a plain < 5 are both accepted. To match the value n already holds, §2's spelling is == n.\"\n\nSo if you write `n when n < 5` where `n` is the parameter, that's rebinding. The guard doesn't exempt you from this. You must write either `m when m < 5` (different name) or `== n` (explicit match).\n\nBut in c07, it appears to be correct - they ARE using a different name `m` in... wait no. Let me look again:\n\n```csharp\nClassify(n) -> n switch {\n    n when n < 5 => :retried,\n    >= 5         => :exhausted\n}\n```\n\nThe pattern of the first arm is `n`, which IS a bare name already in scope. So this SHOULD be reported as `rebinding`. Let me check what the expected output should be by reasoning:\n\nThe code has `n when n < 5` where `n` is the parameter. According to the spec, this should be `rebinding`. So maybe c07 is NOT a test case for \"no errors\" but rather for detecting a specific error?\n\nLet me look at the case directory name again: `c07-guarded`. Let me reconsider - maybe the case is meant to test guarded patterns and whether I handle them correctly for exhaustiveness checking?\n\nLet me look at c05 and c12 more carefully:","time":{"start":1788997798767,"end":1788997805684}}}
