{"type":"text","timestamp":1788997815526,"sessionID":"ses_f776f37c6ffeLHNTo5P39JtFVe","part":{"id":"prt_088943b26001OeTFA8zyGu4Do9","messageID":"msg_088943773001LsZAla5duxZbMC","sessionID":"ses_f776f37c6ffeLHNTo5P39JtFVe","type":"text","text":"So c12 has a vacuous arm: `(:pass, n)` is a tuple pattern, but `Verdict` is `:pass | :fail`, which are atoms, not tuples. So the first arm is vacuous.\n\nBut I'm also reporting `return_not_declared` for some reason. Let me check: `n` is an int variable bound by the pattern, but it's used directly as the return value. But the return type is `int`, and `n` is bound as... well, there's no type annotation, so I assume it's `term`? Actually, the return type is `int`, so if we're returning `n`, it must be that `n` is an int. But since the pattern is vacuous, this doesn't matter.\n\nThe issue is that I'm checking return types before checking vacuity. Let me reorder my checks so that if an arm is vacuous, I don't check anything else about it.\n\nActually, wait. Let me think about this differently. The specification says we can report at most 9 tags. Each case can have multiple tags (in the order they're discovered). Let me think about what the reference implementation would do:\n\nFor c12:\n1. First arm `(:pass, n) => n` is vacuous (pattern doesn't match type), so report `vacuous_arm`\n2. Rest of arms are fine\n\nSo we should only report `vacuous_arm`, not `return_not_declared`.\n\nThe issue is that when an arm is vacuous, we shouldn't check other properties of it. Let me fix this:","time":{"start":1788997810982,"end":1788997815512}}}
