{"type":"text","timestamp":1788997820768,"sessionID":"ses_f776f37c6ffeLHNTo5P39JtFVe","part":{"id":"prt_0889452f6001DCFx5aykXdMYDD","messageID":"msg_088944ede001Vn5wfWJgOUO13g","sessionID":"ses_f776f37c6ffeLHNTo5P39JtFVe","type":"text","text":"In c09:\n- `Check` takes `int n`\n- The first arm pattern is `m`, which binds `m` to the input (int)\n- The guard calls `Big(m)` which expects `int` - OK\n- The body calls `Tag(m)` which expects `atom` but gets `int` - ERROR!\n\nSo it should report `arg_not_accepted`. But I'm reporting `return_not_declared`. Let me debug this - I probably have the order wrong or I'm mishandling the return type check.\n\nActually wait, looking at my code for `check_return_types`, I'm checking if the body value could be the return type. For `Tag(m)`, the return type of `Tag` is `atom`, and the declared return type of `Check` is `atom`, so that should be fine. Why am I reporting it as wrong?\n\nLet me trace through my logic... Actually, I see the issue. In `value_could_be_type`, I'm checking if a function call's return type matches. But I think the issue is that I'm not properly checking `Tag(m)`. Let me add some debug output:\n\nActually, I think the real issue is that I need to be more careful about the order of checks. Let me completely rewrite the checker with proper ordering:","time":{"start":1788997817078,"end":1788997820753}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788997873887,"sessionID":"ses_f776f37c6ffeLHNTo5P39JtFVe","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"toolu_015zDCshoVijG3ceE8rUJZmF","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"\nSwitch exhaustiveness checker for .bs language.\n\"\"\"\nimport sys\nimport re\nfrom typing import List, Dict, Set, Tuple, Optional\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass, field\n\n@dataclass\nclass Type:\n    name: str\n    variants: List[str] = field(default_factory=list)\n    \n    def is_closed(self) -> bool:\n        return len(self.variants) > 0\n\n@dataclass\nclass FunctionDef:\n    name: str\n    param_type: str\n    return_type: str\n    param_names: List[str] = field(default_factory=list)\n    clauses: List[Tuple[str, Optional[str]]] = field(default_factory=list)\n\n@dataclass  \nclass SwitchArm:\n    pattern: str\n    guard: Optional[str]\n    body: str\n    index: int\n\nclass Parser:\n    def __init__(self, content: str):\n        self.content = content\n        self.lines = content.split('\\n')\n        self.types: Dict[str, Type] = {}\n        self.functions: Dict[str, FunctionDef] = {}\n        self.switches: Dict[str, List[SwitchArm]] = {}\n        self.parse()\n    \n    def parse(self):\n        i = 0\n        while i < len(self.lines):\n            line = self.lines[i].strip()\n            \n            if not line or line.startswith('module'):\n                i += 1\n                continue\n            \n            if line.startswith('type '):\n                self.parse_type_def(line)\n                i += 1\n            elif line.startswith('public '):\n                i = self.parse_function(i)\n            else:\n                i += 1\n    \n    def parse_type_def(self, line: str):\n        match = re.match(r'type\\s+(\\w+)\\s*=\\s*(.*)', line)\n        if match:\n            name = match.group(1)\n            variants_str = match.group(2)\n            variants = [v.strip() for v in variants_str.split('|')]\n            self.types[name] = Type(name, variants)\n    \n    def parse_function(self, line_idx: int) -> int:\n        sig_line = self.lines[line_idx].strip()\n        match = re.match(r'public\\s+(\\S+)\\s+(\\w+)\\s*\\((.*)\\)', sig_line)\n        if not match:\n            return line_idx + 1\n        \n        return_type = match.group(1)\n        func_name = match.group(2)\n        params_str = match.group(3)\n        \n        param_types = []\n        param_names = []\n        \n        if ',' in params_str:\n            parts = params_str.split(',')\n            for part in parts:\n                parts2 = part.strip().split()\n                if len(parts2) >= 2:\n                    param_types.append(parts2[0])\n                    param_names.append(parts2[1])\n        else:\n            parts = params_str.strip().split()\n            if len(parts) >= 2:\n                param_types.append(parts[0])\n                param_names.append(parts[1])\n        \n        if len(param_types) == 1:\n            param_type = param_types[0]\n        else:\n            param_type = f\"({','.join(param_types)})\"\n        \n        func_def = FunctionDef(func_name, param_type, return_type, param_names)\n        self.functions[func_name] = func_def\n        \n        idx = line_idx + 1\n        while idx < len(self.lines):\n            line = self.lines[idx].strip()\n            if not line:\n                idx += 1\n                continue\n            \n            if line.startswith(func_name + '('):\n                idx = self.parse_clause_and_switch(func_name, idx, func_def)\n            else:\n                break\n        \n        return idx\n    \n    def parse_clause_and_switch(self, func_name: str, line_idx: int, func_def: FunctionDef) -> int:\n        clause_line = self.lines[line_idx].strip()\n        match = re.match(r'(\\w+)\\((.*?)\\)\\s*(?:when\\s+(.+?))?\\s*->', clause_line)\n        if not match:\n            return line_idx + 1\n        \n        pattern = match.group(2)\n        guard = match.group(3).strip() if match.group(3) else None\n        \n        func_def.clauses.append((pattern, guard))\n        \n        idx = line_idx\n        while idx < len(self.lines):\n            if 'switch' in self.lines[idx]:\n                idx = self.parse_switch(func_name, idx)\n                break\n            idx += 1\n        \n        return idx\n    \n    def parse_switch(self, func_name: str, start_idx: int) -> int:\n        switch_text = \"\"\n        idx = start_idx\n        \n        while idx < len(self.lines):\n            switch_text += self.lines[idx] + \" \"\n            if '}' in self.lines[idx]:\n                idx += 1\n                break\n            idx += 1\n        \n        arms_match = re.search(r'switch\\s*\\{(.*)\\}', switch_text, re.DOTALL)\n        if not arms_match:\n            return idx\n        \n        arms_text = arms_match.group(1)\n        arms = self.split_switch_arms(arms_text)\n        \n        if func_name not in self.switches:\n            self.switches[func_name] = []\n        \n        for i, arm_text in enumerate(arms):\n            arm_text = arm_text.strip()\n            if not arm_text:\n                continue\n            \n            arm_match = re.match(r'(.*?)\\s*(?:when\\s+(.+?))?\\s*=>\\s*(.*)', arm_text)\n            if arm_match:\n                pattern = arm_match.group(1).strip()\n                guard = arm_match.group(2).strip() if arm_match.group(2) else None\n                body = arm_match.group(3).strip()\n                \n                switch_arm = SwitchArm(pattern, guard, body, i)\n                self.switches[func_name].append(switch_arm)\n        \n        return idx\n    \n    def split_switch_arms(self, text: str) -> List[str]:\n        arms = []\n        current = \"\"\n        depth = 0\n        \n        for char in text:\n            if char in '({[':\n                depth += 1\n            elif char in ')}]':\n                depth -= 1\n            elif char == ',' and depth == 0:\n                arms.append(current)\n                current = \"\"\n                continue\n            \n            current += char\n        \n        if current:\n            arms.append(current)\n        \n        return arms\n\nclass Checker:\n    def __init__(self, parser: Parser):\n        self.parser = parser\n        self.diagnostics: List[str] = []\n    \n    def check(self):\n        for func_name, arms in self.parser.switches.items():\n            func_def = self.parser.functions.get(func_name)\n            if not func_def:\n                continue\n            \n            # Check in specific order\n            self.check_switch_in_guard(func_name, func_def)\n            self.check_vacuous_arms(func_name, arms, func_def)\n            self.check_unreachable_arms(func_name, arms, func_def)\n            self.check_unsatisfiable_guards(func_name, arms)\n            self.check_rebinding(func_name, arms, func_def)\n            self.check_unbound_variables(func_name, arms, func_def)\n            self.check_arg_not_accepted(func_name, arms, func_def)\n            self.check_return_types(func_name, arms, func_def)\n            self.check_exhaustiveness(func_name, arms, func_def)\n    \n    def check_switch_in_guard(self, func_name: str, func_def: FunctionDef):\n        for pattern, guard in func_def.clauses:\n            if guard and 'switch' in guard:\n                self.diagnostics.append('switch_in_guard')\n    \n    def check_vacuous_arms(self, func_name: str, arms: List[SwitchArm], func_def: FunctionDef):\n        \"\"\"Check for arms that don't match the subject type\"\"\"\n        param_type = func_def.param_type\n        type_def = self.parser.types.get(param_type)\n        \n        if not type_def:\n            return\n        \n        for arm in arms:\n            pattern = arm.pattern.strip()\n            \n            if pattern == '_':\n                continue\n            \n            # Check if pattern could match a variant of the type\n            is_valid = False\n            for variant in type_def.variants:\n                if self.pattern_could_match_variant(pattern, variant):\n                    is_valid = True\n                    break\n            \n            if not is_valid:\n                self.diagnostics.append('vacuous_arm')\n    \n    def check_unreachable_arms(self, func_name: str, arms: List[SwitchArm], func_def: FunctionDef):\n        \"\"\"Check for arms covered by earlier arms\"\"\"\n        for i in range(1, len(arms)):\n            current = arms[i]\n            current_pattern = current.pattern.strip()\n            \n            for j in range(i):\n                earlier = arms[j]\n                earlier_pattern = earlier.pattern.strip()\n                \n                if self.pattern_covers(earlier_pattern, current_pattern):\n                    self.diagnostics.append('unreachable_arm')\n                    return\n    \n    def check_unsatisfiable_guards(self, func_name: str, arms: List[SwitchArm]):\n        \"\"\"Check for guards that can never be satisfied\"\"\"\n        for arm in arms:\n            if arm.guard:\n                if not self.is_guard_satisfiable(arm.guard):\n                    self.diagnostics.append('unsatisfiable_arm_guard')\n                    return\n    \n    def check_rebinding(self, func_name: str, arms: List[SwitchArm], func_def: FunctionDef):\n        \"\"\"Check for rebinding of clause parameters in arm patterns\"\"\"\n        for arm in arms:\n            # Get bare names in pattern\n            pattern_names = self.extract_bare_names_from_pattern(arm.pattern)\n            \n            for name in pattern_names:\n                if name in func_def.param_names:\n                    self.diagnostics.append('rebinding')\n                    return\n    \n    def check_unbound_variables(self, func_name: str, arms: List[SwitchArm], func_def: FunctionDef):\n        \"\"\"Check for unbound variables in arm bodies\"\"\"\n        for arm in arms:\n            # Skip vacuous arms\n            if self.is_arm_vacuous(arm, func_def):\n                continue\n            \n            # Bound variables: parameter names + arm pattern bindings\n            bound = set(func_def.param_names)\n            bound.update(self.extract_bound_vars(arm.pattern))\n            \n            # Used variables in body\n            used = self.extract_vars_used_in_body(arm.body)\n            \n            for var in used:\n                if var not in bound:\n                    self.diagnostics.append('unbound_variable')\n                    return\n    \n    def check_arg_not_accepted(self, func_name: str, arms: List[SwitchArm], func_def: FunctionDef):\n        \"\"\"Check if function calls receive correct types\"\"\"\n        for arm in arms:\n            # Skip vacuous arms\n            if self.is_arm_vacuous(arm, func_def):\n                continue\n            \n            body = arm.body.strip()\n            \n            # Find function calls\n            calls = re.findall(r'(\\w+)\\s*\\(([^)]*)\\)', body)\n            for called_name, args_str in calls:\n                called_func = self.parser.functions.get(called_name)\n                if not called_func:\n                    continue\n                \n                # Get first argument\n                if not args_str:\n                    continue\n                \n                arg = args_str.split(',')[0].strip()\n                \n                # Infer argument type\n                arg_type = self.infer_type(arg, arm, func_def)\n                \n                # Check compatibility\n                if not self.types_compatible(arg_type, called_func.param_type):\n                    self.diagnostics.append('arg_not_accepted')\n                    return\n    \n    def check_return_types(self, func_name: str, arms: List[SwitchArm], func_def: FunctionDef):\n        \"\"\"Check if return values match declared type\"\"\"\n        for arm in arms:\n            # Skip vacuous arms\n            if self.is_arm_vacuous(arm, func_def):\n                continue\n            \n            body = arm.body.strip()\n            \n            if not self.value_matches_type(body, func_def.return_type, func_def, arm):\n                self.diagnostics.append('return_not_declared')\n                return\n    \n    def check_exhaustiveness(self, func_name: str, arms: List[SwitchArm], func_def: FunctionDef):\n        \"\"\"Check if switch covers all cases\"\"\"\n        param_type = func_def.param_type\n        type_def = self.parser.types.get(param_type)\n        \n        has_catchall = any(a.pattern.strip() == '_' for a in arms)\n        \n        if type_def and type_def.is_closed():\n            if has_catchall:\n                # Catch-all on closed type is illegal, but we're checking exhaustiveness\n                return\n            \n            # Count covered variants\n            covered = set()\n            for arm in arms:\n                for variant in type_def.variants:\n                    if self.arm_covers_variant(arm, variant):\n                        covered.add(variant)\n            \n            if len(covered) < len(type_def.variants):\n                self.diagnostics.append('switch_inexhaustive')\n        else:\n            # Open type - need catch-all or exhaustive patterns\n            if not has_catchall and param_type.startswith('('):\n                # Tuple type - check exhaustiveness\n                if not self.is_tuple_exhaustive(arms, param_type):\n                    self.diagnostics.append('switch_inexhaustive')\n    \n    # Helper methods\n    \n    def is_arm_vacuous(self, arm: SwitchArm, func_def: FunctionDef) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Check if this arm's pattern is vacuous for the subject type\"\"\"\n        param_type = func_def.param_type\n        type_def = self.parser.types.get(param_type)\n        \n        if not type_def:\n            return False\n        \n        pattern = arm.pattern.strip()\n        if pattern == '_':\n            return False\n        \n        for variant in type_def.variants:\n            if self.pattern_could_match_variant(pattern, variant):\n                return False\n        \n        return True\n    \n    def extract_bare_names_from_pattern(self, pattern: str) -> Set[str]:\n        \"\"\"Extract bare identifiers from a pattern (excludes == patterns)\"\"\"\n        # Remove == patterns\n        pattern_no_eq = re.sub(r'==\\s*\\w+', '', pattern)\n        \n        # Extract identifiers\n        names = set()\n        tokens = re.findall(r'[a-z_]\\w*', pattern_no_eq)\n        \n        for token in tokens:\n            if token and not token.startswith(':'):\n                names.add(token)\n        \n        return names\n    \n    def extract_bound_vars(self, pattern: str) -> Set[str]:\n        \"\"\"Extract variables bound by this pattern\"\"\"\n        # This includes bare names, not == patterns\n        bound = set()\n        \n        # Get bare names\n        pattern_no_eq = re.sub(r'==\\s*\\w+', '', pattern)\n        tokens = re.findall(r'[a-z_]\\w*', pattern_no_eq)\n        \n        for token in tokens:\n            if token and not token.startswith(':'):\n                bound.add(token)\n        \n        return bound\n    \n    def extract_vars_used_in_body(self, body: str) -> Set[str]:\n        \"\"\"Extract variables used in a function body\"\"\"\n        used = set()\n        \n        # Find identifiers that are NOT:\n        # - preceded by : (atoms)\n        # - function names (followed by ()\n        # - keywords\n        \n        # Simple approach: find word boundaries and filter\n        tokens = re.findall(r'\\b([a-z_]\\w*)\\b', body)\n        \n        for token in tokens:\n            if token in ['switch', 'when', 'and', 'or', 'true', 'false']:\n                continue\n            if token.startswith(':'):\n                continue\n            used.add(token)\n        \n        return used\n    \n    def pattern_could_match_variant(self, pattern: str, variant: str) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Can pattern match this specific variant?\"\"\"\n        pattern = pattern.strip()\n        \n        if pattern == '_':\n            return True\n        \n        if pattern == variant:\n            return True\n        \n        # Tuple patterns can match tuple variants\n        if '(' in pattern and '(' in variant:\n            return True\n        \n        return False\n    \n    def pattern_covers(self, p1: str, p2: str) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Does pattern p1 cover pattern p2?\"\"\"\n        p1 = p1.strip()\n        p2 = p2.strip()\n        \n        if p1 == '_':\n            return True\n        \n        if p1 == p2:\n            return True\n        \n        return False\n    \n    def is_guard_satisfiable(self, guard: str) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Can this guard be satisfied?\"\"\"\n        parts = guard.split(' and ')\n        \n        if len(parts) < 2:\n            return True\n        \n        # Check for contradictions\n        for i in range(len(parts)):\n            for j in range(i + 1, len(parts)):\n                if self.are_contradictory(parts[i].strip(), parts[j].strip()):\n                    return False\n        \n        return True\n    \n    def are_contradictory(self, c1: str, c2: str) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Do these conditions contradict each other?\"\"\"\n        # x > N and x < M where N >= M\n        m1 = re.match(r'(\\w+)\\s*>\\s*(\\d+)', c1)\n        m2 = re.match(r'(\\w+)\\s*<\\s*(\\d+)', c2)\n        \n        if m1 and m2:\n            var1, val1 = m1.group(1), int(m1.group(2))\n            var2, val2 = m2.group(1), int(m2.group(2))\n            if var1 == var2 and val1 >= val2:\n                return True\n        \n        return False\n    \n    def infer_type(self, arg: str, arm: SwitchArm, context_func: FunctionDef) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Infer the type of an argument expression\"\"\"\n        arg = arg.strip()\n        \n        # If it's a parameter\n        if arg in context_func.param_names:\n            return context_func.param_type\n        \n        # If it's a literal\n        if arg.startswith(':'):\n            return 'atom'\n        if arg.isdigit() or (arg.startswith('-') and arg[1:].isdigit()):\n            return 'int'\n        if arg in ['true', 'false']:\n            return 'bool'\n        \n        # If it's a function call\n        if '(' in arg:\n            func_name = arg.split('(')[0]\n            func = self.parser.functions.get(func_name)\n            if func:\n                return func.return_type\n        \n        # If it's a variable bound by the pattern\n        if arg in self.extract_bound_vars(arm.pattern):\n            # Try to infer from pattern\n            return 'term'\n        \n        return 'term'\n    \n    def types_compatible(self, actual: str, expected: str) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Is actual compatible with expected?\"\"\"\n        if expected == 'term':\n            return True\n        \n        return actual == expected\n    \n    def value_matches_type(self, value: str, type_name: str, func_def: FunctionDef, arm: SwitchArm) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Does this value match the declared type?\"\"\"\n        value = value.strip()\n        \n        if type_name == 'atom':\n            if value.startswith(':'):\n                return True\n            # Function call returning atom\n            if '(' in value:\n                func_name = value.split('(')[0]\n                func = self.parser.functions.get(func_name)\n                if func and func.return_type == 'atom':\n                    return True\n            return False\n        \n        elif type_name == 'int':\n            if value.isdigit() or (value.startswith('-') and value[1:].isdigit()):\n                return True\n            # Function call returning int\n            if '(' in value:\n                func_name = value.split('(')[0]\n                func = self.parser.functions.get(func_name)\n                if func and func.return_type == 'int':\n                    return True\n            # Variable - could be int\n            if value in self.extract_bound_vars(arm.pattern):\n                return True\n            return False\n        \n        elif type_name == 'bool':\n            return value in ['true', 'false']\n        \n        elif type_name == 'term':\n            return True\n        \n        return True\n    \n    def arm_covers_variant(self, arm: SwitchArm, variant: str) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Does this arm cover the variant?\"\"\"\n        pattern = arm.pattern.strip()\n        \n        if pattern == '_':\n            return True\n        \n        if pattern == variant:\n            return True\n        \n        return False\n    \n    def is_tuple_exhaustive(self, arms: List[SwitchArm], tuple_type: str) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Is this tuple switch exhaustive?\"\"\"\n        # Parse tuple type like \"(bool, bool, bool)\"\n        inner = tuple_type.strip('()').split(',')\n        element_types = [t.strip() for t in inner]\n        \n        # For bool tuples, enumerate all cases\n        if all(t == 'bool' for t in element_types):\n            total_cases = 2 ** len(element_types)\n            \n            # Generate all possible bool tuples\n            all_cases = self.generate_bool_tuples(len(element_types))\n            \n            covered_cases = set()\n            for arm in arms:\n                pattern = arm.pattern.strip()\n                if pattern == '_':\n                    return True\n                \n                # Find all cases this pattern covers\n                for case in all_cases:\n                    if self.tuple_pattern_matches(pattern, case):\n                        covered_cases.add(case)\n            \n            return len(covered_cases) == total_cases\n        \n        return False\n    \n    def generate_bool_tuples(self, n: int) -> List[Tuple[bool, ...]]:\n        \"\"\"Generate all possible bool tuples of length n\"\"\"\n        if n == 0:\n            return [()]\n        \n        sub = self.generate_bool_tuples(n - 1)\n        result = []\n        for s in sub:\n            result.append(s + (True,))\n            result.append(s + (False,))\n        \n        return result\n    \n    def tuple_pattern_matches(self, pattern: str, case: Tuple[bool, ...]) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Does this pattern match this specific bool tuple?\"\"\"\n        pattern = pattern.strip()\n        pattern = pattern.strip('()')\n        \n        parts = [p.strip() for p in pattern.split(',')]\n        \n        if len(parts) != len(case):\n            return False\n        \n        for i, part in enumerate(parts):\n            if part == '_':\n                continue\n            \n            # Check if part matches case[i]\n            case_str = 'true' if case[i] else 'false'\n            if part != case_str:\n                return False\n        \n        return True\n\ndef main():\n    if len(sys.argv) < 2:\n        sys.exit(1)\n    \n    filepath = sys.argv[1]\n    \n    try:\n        with open(filepath, 'r') as f:\n            content = f.read()\n    except:\n        sys.exit(1)\n    \n    parser = Parser(content)\n    checker = Checker(parser)\n    checker.check()\n    \n    # Output sorted unique diagnostics\n    for diag in sorted(set(checker.diagnostics)):\n        print(diag)\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    main()\n","filePath":"/Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-10-round4/copilot-haiku45/switchcheck"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-10-round4/copilot-haiku45/switchcheck","exists":true,"truncated":false},"title":"Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-10-round4/copilot-haiku45/switchcheck","time":{"start":1788997873682,"end":1788997873856}},"metadata":{"anthropic":{"caller":{"type":"direct"}}},"id":"prt_08894615c001mRX2uxldTKRlzt","sessionID":"ses_f776f37c6ffeLHNTo5P39JtFVe","messageID":"msg_088944ede001Vn5wfWJgOUO13g"}}
