{"type":"text","timestamp":1788997934565,"sessionID":"ses_f776f37c6ffeLHNTo5P39JtFVe","part":{"id":"prt_08895f5f2001Aor1T4UV2iwYRz","messageID":"msg_08895f1a5001GKkpmevCMl7TIp","sessionID":"ses_f776f37c6ffeLHNTo5P39JtFVe","type":"text","text":"Hmm, no rebinding test case name. Let me check which cases are supposed to have which errors by looking at the case names:\n\n- c01-exhaustive-closed: no error\n- c02-catchall-open: no error\n- c03-inexhaustive: inexhaustive\n- c04-unreachable: unreachable\n- c05-tuple-exhaustive: no error\n- c06-tuple-inexhaustive: inexhaustive\n- c07-guarded: ? (guarded - testing guards work correctly)\n- c08-wrong-return: return error\n- c09-arg-not-accepted: arg error\n- c10-switch-in-guard: switch in guard error\n- c11-unbound-variable: unbound error\n- c12-vacuous-arm: vacuous error\n- c13-unsatisfiable-guard: unsatisfiable guard error\n\nSo c07 is \"guarded\" - which probably means it's testing that GUARDED PATTERNS work correctly. So it shouldn't have an error. That means my understanding is wrong.\n\nLet me re-read the spec one more time, very carefully...\n\nOh wait! I just realized something. When the spec says \"A bare name in a pattern *introduces* a name\", maybe it means specifically a BARE name, i.e., a name with no operators or qualifiers. And maybe `== n` is also a bare name in some sense?\n\nActually, let me look at the spec's rule about `== n`:\n\n\"To match against a value a name already holds, write `== name`. A bare name in a pattern introduces a name; `== name` matches the value that name is bound to.\"\n\nSo:\n- Bare name (just `n`) → introduces a new binding\n- `== name` → matches against existing binding\n\nNow the question: does `n when Guard` use `n` as a bare name binding, or as something else?\n\nReading further: \"A bare `n` as an arm pattern is rejected with no guard present at all; `m when m < 5` and a plain `< 5` are both accepted.\"\n\nSo:\n- `n` alone is rejected (bare name binding)\n- `n when n < 5` is... unclear. It says it's rejected but doesn't this fall under the same category?\n\nWait, let me read the exact text again: \"A bare `n` as an arm pattern is rejected with no guard present at all\". This phrase \"with no guard present at all\" suggests that maybe WITH a guard, it's different?\n\nActually, I think I'm misreading the grammar. Let me parse it carefully:\n\n\"A bare `n` as an arm pattern is rejected with no guard present at all\"\n\nCould mean:\n- Interpretation A: \"A bare n is rejected. [Separately, something else happens] with no guard.\"\n- Interpretation B: \"A bare n is rejected [only in cases] with no guard present at all [i.e., when there is a guard, it's accepted]\"\n\nIf interpretation B is correct, then `n when n < 5` would be OK! And that would match why c07 is testing \"guarded\" patterns.\n\nBut that seems wrong based on the earlier statement \"The guard is not what makes it an error.\"\n\nHmm, let me look at what's accepted: \"`m when m < 5` and a plain `< 5` are both accepted.\"\n\nSo both of these are accepted. `m when m < 5` uses a fresh name. But what if you write `n when n < 5`? The statement says \"The guard is not what makes it an error\", which implies that if there IS an error, it's not because of the guard. So the error is the bare name binding.\n\nOK so I think c07 SHOULD output `rebinding`. Unless there's something subtle about the test setup that I'm missing...\n\nLet me look at test c09 which I'm currently failing on:","time":{"start":1788997924338,"end":1788997934553}}}
