{"type":"text","timestamp":1788997953053,"sessionID":"ses_f776f37c6ffeLHNTo5P39JtFVe","part":{"id":"prt_088964a99001RCErxgqZv7m80Y","messageID":"msg_08896458d001a64O3bZ2pCgc6C","sessionID":"ses_f776f37c6ffeLHNTo5P39JtFVe","type":"text","text":"Let me trace this manually. The body is `Tag(m)`. My regex `r'(\\w+)\\s*\\(([^)]*)\\)'` should match this and give me `('Tag', 'm')`. Then I extract the first arg as 'm'. Then I infer its type based on the arm pattern and context.\n\nThe arm pattern is `m`, which binds `m` to the switch subject (which is `n` of type `int` from the Check parameter). So `m` should have type `int`.\n\nThen I check if `int` is compatible with `atom` (Tag's parameter type). They're not, so I should report `arg_not_accepted`.\n\nBut instead I'm getting `return_not_declared`. This suggests that the arg checking isn't working. Let me add some debug output to the actual script:\n\nActually, let me think about this differently. Maybe my `infer_type` function is wrong? Let me look at it:\n\n```python\ndef infer_type(self, arg: str, arm: SwitchArm, context_func: FunctionDef) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Infer the type of an argument expression\"\"\"\n    arg = arg.strip()\n    \n    # If it's a parameter\n    if arg in context_func.param_names:\n        return context_func.param_type\n    \n    # ... more cases ...\n    \n    # If it's a variable bound by the pattern\n    if arg in self.extract_bound_vars(arm.pattern):\n        # Try to infer from pattern\n        return 'term'\n    \n    return 'term'\n```\n\nSo if `m` is bound by the pattern `m`, I return 'term'. But that's wrong - I should return the type of the switch subject! The switch subject is the thing being switched on, which is the parameter `n` of type `int`.\n\nThat's the bug! When I bind a variable through a pattern, its type is the type of the thing being matched. In a switch, the thing being matched is the switch subject. In this case, the switch is `n switch {... }`, so the switch subject is `n` which has type `int`.\n\nI need to know what the switch subject is. Looking at the parsed clause, I have the clause pattern but not the switch subject. Let me look at the code:\n\nIn `parse_clause_and_switch`, I get the clause pattern but then I parse the switch body. I need to extract the switch subject from the switch statement itself.\n\nLooking at c09's structure:\n```\nCheck(n) -> n switch { ... }\n```\n\nThe switch subject is `n`. I need to parse this. Let me update the parser to extract the switch subject:","time":{"start":1788997946009,"end":1788997953048}}}
