(m)`. That's where I should find the function call.\n\nSo check_arg_not_accepted should find:\n- calls = [('Tag', 'm')]\n- called_name = 'Tag'\n- called_func = Tag function (return type atom, param type atom)\n- arg = 'm'\n- arg_type = infer_type('m', arm, func_def)\n\nNow, `infer_type` should return the type of 'm'. Looking at my updated function:\n- 'm' is in arm.pattern (the pattern binds 'm')\n- arm.subject should be 'n' (extracted from `n switch { ... }`)\n- 'n' is in context_func.param_names (it's the parameter of Check)\n- context_func.param_type is 'int'\n\nSo infer_type should return 'int'.\n\nThen types_compatible('int', 'atom') should return False.\n\nSo I should append 'arg_not_accepted'.\n\nUnless... maybe the subject extraction isn't working? Let me check the regex:\n\n```python\nsubject_match = re.search(r'(\\w+)\\s+switch\\s*\\{', switch_text)\n```\n\nFor `n switch {`, this should match and capture 'n'.\n\nActually, looking at c09 more carefully - maybe the issue is that switch_text doesn't contain just `n switch { ... }` but also has other stuff before it?\n\nLooking at c09's Check clause:\n```\nCheck(n) -> n switch {\n    m when Big(m) => Tag(m),\n    _             => :small\n}\n```\n\nSo switch_text should include \"n switch {\" and should match. But wait, there's a guard in the first arm: `m when Big(m) => Tag(m)`.\n\nActually, the guard is part of the arm, not the clause! Let me re-read the clause structure:\n\n```\nClassify(n) -> n switch {\n    n when n < 5 => :retried,\n    >= 5         => :exhausted\n}\n```\n\nSo:\n- Clause pattern: `n`\n- Clause guard: none\n- Clause body: `n switch { ... }`\n\nAnd the switch arms have their own guards.\n\nSo the guard `when Big(m)` is an ARM guard, not a clause guard. So it's in `arm.guard`, not in the clause guard.\n\nBut my check_arg_not_accepted doesn't check arm guards! I only check the body. Let me look again:\n\n```python\ncalls = re.findall(r'(\\w+)\\s*\\(([^)]*)\\)', body)\n```\n\nSo I'm only finding calls in the body, not in the guard. But the call `Big(m)` is in the guard!\n\nHowever, that's not where the arg_not_accepted error is. The error is `Tag(m)` in the body, and Tag expects atom but gets int.\n\nSo I should be finding Tag and reporting the error. Let me think about why I'm not...\n\nActually, wait. Maybe the issue is that the code IS correctly finding the error and reporting arg_not_accepted, but then check_return_types is ALSO running and reporting return_not_declared, and that's overwriting it?\n\nNo, I use `return` after appending a diagnostic, so I should stop after the first error.\n\nLet me add some actual debug output to the script. Actually, let me try a different approach - let me manually run through the code:\n\nActually, I think I should try running the checker on c09 and see what happens. Let me first make sure the file runs without errors:","time":{"start":1788998013828,"end":1788998029430}}}
