{"type":"text","timestamp":1788998047972,"sessionID":"ses_f776f37c6ffeLHNTo5P39JtFVe","part":{"id":"prt_08897bf5e001otW0xKNZgJw5TW","messageID":"msg_08897ba8f001enFKlv2Z5Rbu9H","sessionID":"ses_f776f37c6ffeLHNTo5P39JtFVe","type":"text","text":"Good, so I do `i = self.parse_function(i)`, which should continue parsing from where parse_function returns. Let me trace through what happens:\n\n1. Line 0: `module Verdict` - skip\n2. Line 1: `public bool Big...` - parse_function(1)\n   - Creates Big function\n   - Looks at line 2: `Big(n) -> ...` - parse_clause_and_switch\n   - Looks at line 3+: looks for switch, processes it\n   - Returns some index\n3. Line returned: should be the next line after the function\n4. If it's `public atom Tag...`, should parse that\n\nBut looking at my code, parse_function returns idx at line 114. That idx is set from parse_clause_and_switch, which returns some idx from parse_switch.\n\nLooking at parse_switch, it accumulates lines until it finds `}`, then returns idx (which is set after the `}` is found).\n\nSo parse_switch should return the line after the switch. For Big, there's no switch! The body is just `n > 100`. So parse_switch will search for 'switch' and never find it, returning idx which is set in the while loop after processing all lines.\n\nActually, looking at parse_clause_and_switch:\n\n```python\nidx = line_idx\nwhile idx < len(self.lines):\n    if 'switch' in self.lines[idx]:\n        idx = self.parse_switch(func_name, idx)\n        break\n    idx += 1\n\nreturn idx\n```\n\nSo if there's no switch, idx just keeps incrementing until the end of lines. Then it returns that idx (which is len(self.lines)). Then parse_function returns that idx. Then the main loop tries to access line[len(self.lines)], which is out of bounds!\n\nWait no, in the main parse loop, I do `while i < len(self.lines)`, so it should stop.\n\nBut the issue is that Big doesn't have a switch! Big's clause is just `Big(n) -> n > 100`. So when parse_clause_and_switch is called, it looks for 'switch' in the remaining lines and never finds it, so idx becomes len(self.lines), and we're done parsing.\n\nThis is the bug. When a function doesn't have a switch, I shouldn't try to find one. Let me fix this:","time":{"start":1788998041438,"end":1788998047922}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788998054182,"sessionID":"ses_f776f37c6ffeLHNTo5P39JtFVe","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"toolu_01JsX3LVpgVzNejD4KrWLHNZ","state":{"status":"error","input":{"filePath":"/Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-10-round4/copilot-haiku45/switchcheck","newString":"    def parse_clause_and_switch(self, func_name: str, line_idx: int, func_def: FunctionDef) -> int:\n        clause_line = self.lines[line_idx].strip()\n        match = re.match(r'(\\w+)\\((.*?)\\)\\s*(?:when\\s+(.+?))?\\s*->', clause_line)\n        if not match:\n            return line_idx + 1\n        \n        pattern = match.group(2)\n        guard = match.group(3).strip() if match.group(3) else None\n        \n        func_def.clauses.append((pattern, guard))\n        \n        # Look for a switch in the body\n        idx = line_idx + 1\n        found_switch = False\n        while idx < len(self.lines):\n            if 'switch' in self.lines[idx]:\n                idx = self.parse_switch(func_name, idx)\n                found_switch = True\n                break\n            # Stop looking if we hit the next clause or function\n            line_content = self.lines[idx].strip()\n            if line_content and not line_content[0].isspace():\n                if line_content.startswith(func_name + '('):\n                    # Next clause for same function\n                    break\n                elif line_content.startswith('public '):\n                    # Next function\n                    break\n            idx += 1\n        \n        if not found_switch:\n            # No switch found, return the current index\n            idx = line_idx + 1\n            # Skip to the next clause or function\n            while idx < len(self.lines):\n                line_content = self.lines[idx].strip()\n                if line_content.startswith(func_name + '(') or line_content.startswith('public '):\n                    break\n                idx += 1\n        \n        return idx","oldString":"    def parse_clause_and_switch(self, func_name: str, line_idx: int, func_def: FunctionDef) -> int:\n        clause_line = self.lines[line_idx].strip()\n        match = re.match(r'(\\w+)\\((.*?)\\)\\s*(?:when\\s+(.+?))?\\s*->', clause_line)\n        if not match:\n            return line_idx + 1\n        \n        pattern = match.group(2)\n        guard = match.group(3).strip() if match.group(3) else None\n        \n        func_def.clauses.append((pattern, guard))\n        \n        # Now find and parse the switch body\n        idx = line_idx\n        while idx < len(self.lines):\n            if 'switch' in self.lines[idx]:\n                idx = self.parse_switch(func_name, idx)\n                break\n            idx += 1\n        \n        return idx"},"error":"Could not find oldString in the file. It must match exactly, including whitespace, indentation, and line endings.","time":{"start":1788998054043,"end":1788998054148}},"metadata":{"anthropic":{"caller":{"type":"direct"}}},"id":"prt_08897d8b8001l6crTSiq7SxhE2","sessionID":"ses_f776f37c6ffeLHNTo5P39JtFVe","messageID":"msg_08897ba8f001enFKlv2Z5Rbu9H"}}
