{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788998092804,"sessionID":"ses_f776f37c6ffeLHNTo5P39JtFVe","part":{"id":"prt_088988802001ROFuL0MlGxT1vd","messageID":"msg_0889882e30018cRq473x0qYt4A","sessionID":"ses_f776f37c6ffeLHNTo5P39JtFVe","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1788998111449,"sessionID":"ses_f776f37c6ffeLHNTo5P39JtFVe","part":{"id":"prt_088988803001ShdF9z6Q0wqSOV","messageID":"msg_0889882e30018cRq473x0qYt4A","sessionID":"ses_f776f37c6ffeLHNTo5P39JtFVe","type":"text","text":"Now let me tackle c07. Looking at the spec again, the guide says: \"A bare `n` as an arm pattern is rejected with no guard present at all; `m when m < 5` and a plain `< 5` are both accepted. To match the value `n` already holds, §2's spelling is `== n`.\"\n\nThis explicitly lists what's accepted and what's not:\n- Rejected: `n` (bare name rebinding)\n- Accepted: `m when m < 5` (fresh name with guard)\n- Accepted: `< 5` (no name, just guard/relational)\n- Accepted: `== n` (match existing value)\n\nSo `n when n < 5` should be rejected as rebinding. My code correctly detects this.\n\nBut the test case is named \"c07-guarded\" which suggests it's testing guarded patterns. Maybe the test expects NO error, meaning the test file is actually correct?\n\nLet me look more carefully at whether rebinding is always an error or only in certain cases. Looking at the spec's rebinding section:\n\n\"A bare `n` as an arm pattern is rejected with no guard present at all; `m when m < 5` and a plain `< 5` are both accepted.\"\n\nHmm, this could mean: \"A bare n is rejected. With no guard present at all, this is the case; with a guard like `m when m < 5` or plain `< 5`, it's accepted.\"\n\nBut then it says \"The guard is not what makes it an error\", which suggests that even with a guard, a rebinding is still an error.\n\nI think the spec is clear: `n when n < 5` is rebinding and should be rejected. So c07 should output `rebinding`.\n\nBut wait - let me check if maybe the test case name doesn't correspond to what error is expected. Let me look at other test patterns and see if they match the expected output:\n\n- c01-exhaustive-closed → empty ✓\n- c02-catchall-open → empty ✓\n- c03-inexhaustive → switch_inexhaustive ✓\n- c04-unreachable → unreachable_arm ✓\n- c05-tuple-exhaustive → empty ✓\n- c06-tuple-inexhaustive → switch_inexhaustive ✓\n- c07-guarded → ? (my output: rebinding)\n- c08-wrong-return → return_not_declared ✓\n- c09-arg-not-accepted → arg_not_accepted ✓\n- c10-switch-in-guard → my output has switch_in_guard (correct) plus return_not_declared (wrong)\n- c11-unbound-variable → unbound_variable ✓\n- c12-vacuous-arm → vacuous_arm ✓\n- c13-unsatisfiable-guard → my output has unsatisfiable_arm_guard (correct) plus unreachable_arm (wrong)\n\nSo the pattern names seem to match expected errors. So c07-guarded might mean it's testing that guarded patterns are handled correctly, which could mean no error is expected.\n\nLet me re-read the specification one more time to see if there's any ambiguity...\n\nActually, maybe I'm overthinking this. Let me just assume that if the test is named \"c07-guarded\", it probably means guarded patterns work correctly with no error. Perhaps `n when n < 5` is actually allowed?\n\nLet me re-read: \"The name a guarded arm introduces must be fresh — m above, not n.\"\n\nThis is pretty clear - the name must be fresh. So `m when m < 5` is OK, but `n when n < 5` is not OK.\n\nUnless... maybe the specification is showing an INCORRECT example that I should report as an error? Let me look at the section more carefully.\n\nFrom the spec in the switch section:\n```csharp\npublic atom Classify(int n)\n\nClassify(n) -> n switch {\n    m when m < 5 => :retried,\n    >= 5         => :exhausted\n}\n```\n\nThis is labeled as an example in a section about \"Nested inside a record pattern, `{ Deliveries: > 5 }` is not built: a relational pattern goes where a whole argument goes. **shipped** — F7.\"\n\nThen there's another section: \"**An arm's bindings are its own.** A name bound by one arm's pattern is not in scope in another arm's body...\"\n\nAnd in the switch section under \"What the compiler says about a switch\":\n```csharp\npublic atom Pick(int n, term e)\n\nPick(n, e) -> e switch {\n    n => :same,\n    _ => :other\n}\n```\n\nThis is labeled as showing the `rebinding` error.\n\nSo it seems like:\n- `m when m < 5` is correct (fresh name)\n- `n => :same` is rebinding (same name as parameter)\n\nBut what about `n when n < 5`?  The spec says \"The guard is not what makes it an error\", which suggests that `n when n < 5` is STILL rebinding.\n\nSo I think my code is correct to report rebinding for c07. But then why is the test named \"guarded\"?\n\nMaybe the test is actually checking that I DON'T incorrectly report other errors? Like, maybe c07 is checking that I report rebinding (and ONLY rebinding)?\n\nActually, let me try a different interpretation. Maybe c07 has the correct code. Let me read the file again:\n\n```\nClassify(n) -> n switch {\n    n when n < 5 => :retried,\n    >= 5         => :exhausted\n}\n```\n\nHmm, maybe the spec is ambiguous or my interpretation is wrong. Let me check if the guard somehow exempts the name from being treated as a binding.\n\nActually, looking at Erlang semantics (which this language is based on), a pattern like `n when Guard` in a clause would bind `n` to the value. So `n when n < 5` would be rebinding.\n\nBut in the switch arm context, maybe it's different? Let me look for a rule about how guards interact with patterns in arms...\n\nFrom the spec: \"An arm takes the clause head's pattern grammar whole, so it may carry a guard, or be a relational pattern\".\n\nThis says it takes the head's pattern grammar. In a clause head, `n when n < 5` would be rebinding if `n` is already bound. So in an arm, it should also be rebinding.\n\nI'm going to assume my code is correct and c07 should report `rebinding`. If this is wrong, I'll need to revisit it after seeing other results.\n\nNow let me fix c10 and c13. Let me look at c10:","time":{"start":1788998092803,"end":1788998111439}}}
