{"type":"text","timestamp":1788998123741,"sessionID":"ses_f776f37c6ffeLHNTo5P39JtFVe","part":{"id":"prt_08898e226001OjRI0upsQZZMNT","messageID":"msg_08898dd58001P4L7ngEsN2FEwp","sessionID":"ses_f776f37c6ffeLHNTo5P39JtFVe","type":"text","text":"So c10 has TWO clauses for F. The first clause has a switch in the guard, which is the error I'm correctly detecting.\n\nBut I'm also reporting `return_not_declared`. Why?\n\nLet me think... The first clause body is `:yes`, which is an atom. The function's return type is `atom`. So that should match.\n\nThe second clause body is `:no`, which is also an atom and matches.\n\nSo I shouldn't be reporting `return_not_declared`. Let me check if maybe I'm processing something incorrectly...\n\nOh wait! The issue is that I'm checking function switches, but c10's function F has TWO clauses, and only the first one has a switch (in the guard). The second clause is just `F(x) -> :no`, which doesn't have a switch body.\n\nSo when I process this file, I should only find a switch in the first clause's GUARD, not in the body. The guard isn't part of the switch statement body that I'm checking - it's a clause guard.\n\nBut looking at my code, I'm checking `check_switch_in_guard` which looks at clause guards and reports if there's a switch in them. That should work.\n\nThe `return_not_declared` error must be coming from somewhere else. Let me think about what switches I would parse:\n\nLooking at c10, F's first clause is `F(x) when x switch { ... } -> :yes`. This is a clause with a guard that contains a switch. The guard has a switch, so I should report `switch_in_guard`.\n\nBut the clause BODY is `:yes`. There's no `->` followed by a switch expression - the body is just `:yes`.\n\nSo actually, I shouldn't be parsing any switch for F! The only switch is in the guard, and that's not a switch statement that I should analyze for exhaustiveness.\n\nBut my code is reporting `return_not_declared`, which comes from the `check_return_types` function. This function iterates over arms in `self.parser.switches[func_name]`. If `func_name` is not in `self.parser.switches`, the loop doesn't run.\n\nSo the fact that I'm reporting `return_not_declared` means there IS a switch parsed for F. Let me check why...\n\nLooking at the file again: `F(x) when x switch { :a => true, _ => false } -> :yes`\n\nThe clause line has `switch` in it (in the guard). My `parse_clause_and_switch` checks `if 'switch' in clause_line:` and calls `parse_switch`.\n\nThat's the bug! I'm checking if 'switch' appears anywhere in the clause line, but I should only call parse_switch if it's the BODY switch, not a guard switch.\n\nLet me fix this:","time":{"start":1788998115878,"end":1788998123737}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788998131083,"sessionID":"ses_f776f37c6ffeLHNTo5P39JtFVe","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"toolu_0131ZG3exCN9KsrxvuxnouQZ","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-10-round4/copilot-haiku45/switchcheck","newString":"    def parse_clause_and_switch(self, func_name: str, line_idx: int, func_def: FunctionDef) -> int:\n        clause_line = self.lines[line_idx].strip()\n        match = re.match(r'(\\w+)\\((.*?)\\)\\s*(?:when\\s+(.+?))?\\s*->', clause_line)\n        if not match:\n            return line_idx + 1\n        \n        pattern = match.group(2)\n        guard = match.group(3).strip() if match.group(3) else None\n        \n        func_def.clauses.append((pattern, guard))\n        \n        # Check if there's a switch in the clause BODY (after ->), not in the guard\n        arrow_pos = clause_line.find('->')\n        if arrow_pos != -1:\n            body_part = clause_line[arrow_pos + 2:].strip()\n            if 'switch' in body_part:\n                # Switch is on the same line as the clause\n                return self.parse_switch(func_name, line_idx)\n        \n        # Look for a switch in the next few lines (the clause body might be multi-line)\n        idx = line_idx + 1\n        while idx < len(self.lines):\n            line_content = self.lines[idx].strip()\n            \n            # If we hit another clause or public function, stop\n            if line_content.startswith(func_name + '(') or line_content.startswith('public '):\n                return idx\n            \n            # If we find a switch, parse it\n            if 'switch' in line_content:\n                return self.parse_switch(func_name, idx)\n            \n            idx += 1\n        \n        # No switch found, return where we ended\n        return idx","oldString":"    def parse_clause_and_switch(self, func_name: str, line_idx: int, func_def: FunctionDef) -> int:\n        clause_line = self.lines[line_idx].strip()\n        match = re.match(r'(\\w+)\\((.*?)\\)\\s*(?:when\\s+(.+?))?\\s*->', clause_line)\n        if not match:\n            return line_idx + 1\n        \n        pattern = match.group(2)\n        guard = match.group(3).strip() if match.group(3) else None\n        \n        func_def.clauses.append((pattern, guard))\n        \n        # Check if switch is on the same line\n        if 'switch' in clause_line:\n            return self.parse_switch(func_name, line_idx)\n        \n        # Look for a switch in the next few lines\n        idx = line_idx + 1\n        while idx < len(self.lines):\n            line_content = self.lines[idx].strip()\n            \n            # If we hit another clause or public function, stop\n            if line_content.startswith(func_name + '(') or line_content.startswith('public '):\n                return idx\n            \n            # If we find a switch, parse it\n            if 'switch' in line_content:\n                return self.parse_switch(func_name, idx)\n            \n            idx += 1\n        \n        # No switch found, return where we ended\n        return idx"},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-10-round4/copilot-haiku45/switchcheck\n===================================================================\n--- /Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-10-round4/copilot-haiku45/switchcheck\n+++ /Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-10-round4/copilot-haiku45/switchcheck\n@@ -123,13 +123,17 @@\n guard = match.group(3).strip() if match.group(3) else None\n \n func_def.clauses.append((pattern, guard))\n \n-# Check if switch is on the same line\n-if 'switch' in clause_line:\n-    return self.parse_switch(func_name, line_idx)\n+# Check if there's a switch in the clause BODY (after ->), not in the guard\n+arrow_pos = cl