{"type":"text","timestamp":1788998219007,"sessionID":"ses_f776f37c6ffeLHNTo5P39JtFVe","part":{"id":"prt_0889a6665001atcAi01qoGYf7b","messageID":"msg_0889a62c8001ZvJI7aSXd76xFy","sessionID":"ses_f776f37c6ffeLHNTo5P39JtFVe","type":"text","text":"## Summary\n\nAll 13 test cases now pass! Here's what was fixed in this session:\n\n### Key Bug Fixes\n\n1. **Parser loop termination (c07-c13 broken)**: The parser was returning too early when a function didn't have a switch statement in its clause. Fixed by:\n   - Checking if 'switch' appears in the clause BODY (after `->`) rather than anywhere in the line\n   - Properly handling the case where no switch is found by returning the correct index to continue parsing\n\n2. **Missing switch_in_guard check (c10)**: The `check_switch_in_guard` function was only called for functions that had switches in their body. Now it's called for ALL functions to detect switches appearing in clause guards.\n\n3. **False unreachable_arm in c13**: When checking if an arm is unreachable, I was ignoring whether earlier arms had unsatisfiable guards. An arm with an unsatisfiable guard covers nothing, so subsequent arms remain reachable. Added a check to skip unreachable analysis for arms with unsatisfiable guards.\n\n### Final Results\n- **13/13 test cases passing** ✓\n- All 9 diagnostic tags implemented and working correctly\n- Output properly sorted and deduplicated\n- Parser correctly handles multi-clause functions with and without switches\n- All edge cases covered (guarded patterns, tuple exhaustiveness, type mismatches, etc.)","time":{"start":1788998215269,"end":1788998219001}}}
