{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788998245694,"sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"toolu_01U8gKUKcEcfKsoZjnBsnmTY","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-10-round4/copilot-haiku45/PACKET.md","offset":193,"limit":100},"output":"<path>/Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-10-round4/copilot-haiku45/PACKET.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n193: **To match against a value a name already holds, write `== name`.** A bare name in a pattern\n194: introduces a name; `== name` matches the value that name is bound to. **shipped**\n195: \n196: So a head that repeats a bare name — `F(acc, acc)` — is an **error**, not an equality constraint:\n197: both are introductions, and the second rebinds what the first bound, which §1 forbids. `F(acc, ==\n198: acc)` is how you ask for the constraint. This is the whole reason the marker exists; without it the\n199: language has no way to say *the same value again*.\n200: \n201: ```csharp\n202: public int RunLength(int head, list<int> xs)\n203: \n204: RunLength(head, [])                -> 0\n205: RunLength(head, [== head, ..rest]) -> 1 + RunLength(head, rest)\n206: RunLength(head, [_, ..rest])       -> 0\n207: ```\n208: \n209: It is the **equality member of the relational family above**, so a reader who has met `>= 4` in a\n210: head reads `== acc` on sight. The family divides cleanly — **relational operators take a literal,\n211: `==` takes a name** — so `>= acc` is not a span bounded by a runtime value, and `== 4` is not a\n212: second spelling for the literal pattern `4`. Neither is admitted.\n213: \n214: The space is not significant: `==acc` and `== acc` are one program. Written with the space, to match\n215: `>= 4`.\n216: \n217: This is the one capability with no C# equivalent at all — C# patterns cannot match a runtime value,\n218: and push you to `when v == expected`. Here that workaround is worse than it looks, because it moves\n219: a pattern concern into a guard, and the checker reads `var == literal` but not `var == var`: the\n220: guard would credit nothing and the arm would subtract nothing from the residual. **A matched name\n221: credits nothing to the certain set either.** Its value is unknown at compile time, so it may narrow\n222: what is *possible* and never counts as coverage — a `switch` whose only non-catch-all arm matches a\n223: name is inexhaustive over the whole subject type.\n224: \n225: ---\n226: \n227: ---\n228: \n229: ## 3. Exhaustiveness\n230: \n231: **A function that does not cover its declared input does not compile.** No opt-out, no flag. The\n232: **residual is the missing case**, which is why the diagnostic below hands you a clause to paste\n233: rather than a complaint to interpret.\n234: \n235: ```csharp\n236: type Verdict = :positive | :zero | :negative | :unknown\n237: type Reading = (:ok, int) | (:error, atom)\n238: \n239: public Verdict Classify(Reading r)\n240: \n241: Classify((:ok, n)) when n > 0 -> :positive\n242: Classify((:ok, 0))            -> :zero\n243: Classify((:ok, n))            -> :negative\n244: Classify((:error, e))         -> :unknown\n245: ```\n246: \n247: Delete the third clause and the compiler answers:\n248: \n249: ```\n250: error: Classify is not exhaustive\n251:   no clause matches:\n252:     Classify((:ok, n)) when n <= -1 -> ...\n253: ```\n254: \n255: The error is the **missing clause**, not a complaint — the residual is computed exactly and printed\n256: as a head you can paste in. Where it is wide, the **printed** form stops after three cases and says\n257: how many it left; the residual itself is never summarised, and the full one is a query away.\n258: **shipped**, and the truncation **decided**\n259: \n260: A **catch-all is legal only where the residual is open** — over a `term`, or any type with an\n261: unbounded part. Where the compiler knows the remaining case names, `_` is an error: it would put\n262: the language's headline guarantee one character from being switched off invisibly. **shipped** —\n263: and the diagnostic names the discarded cases as a head to write instead.\n264: \n265: ---\n266: \n267: ---\n268: \n269: ## 5. Control flow\n270: \n271: **`switch` is the only branching construct.** There is no `if`, no `else`, no ternary.\n272: \n273: <!-- check:\n274: type Verdict = :new | :gone | :unknown\n275: record Order { Id: int, Status: atom }\n276: -->\n277: ```csharp\n278: public Verdict Describe(Order o)\n279: \n280: Describe(o) -> o.Status switch {\n281:     :placed  => :new,\n282:     :shipped => :gone,\n283:     _        => :unknown\n284: }\n285: ```\n286: \n287: The `_` here is legal because `Status` is an `atom` and the atom universe is open, so the residual\n288: cannot be enumerated — which is the only shape a catch-all is admitted over. Over a *closed*\n289: residual, where the compiler knows the missing case by name, §2 makes `_` an error telling you to\n290: name it — **shipped**, at a switch arm and at a clause head alike.\n291: \n292: For compound conditions, the subject is a **tuple** — which is the clause head's own shape, one\n\n(Showing lines 193-292 of 506. Use offset=293 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"**To match against a value a name already holds, write `== name`.** A bare name in a pattern\nintroduces a name; `== name` matches the value that name is bound to. **shipped**\n\nSo a head that repeats a bare name — `F(acc, acc)` — is an **error**, not an equality constraint:\nboth are introductions, and the second rebinds what the first bound, which §1 forbids. `F(acc, ==\nacc)` is how you ask for the constraint. This is the whole reason the marker exists; without it the\nlanguage has no way to say *the same value again*.\n\n```csharp\npublic int RunLength(int head, list<int> xs)\n\nRunLength(head, [])                -> 0\nRunLength(head, [== head, ..rest]) -> 1 + RunLength(head, rest)\nRunLength(head, [_, ..rest])       -> 0\n```\n\nIt is the **equality member of the relational family above**, so a reader who has met `>= 4` in a\nhead reads `== acc` on sight. The family divides cleanly — **relational operators take a literal,\n`==` takes a name** — so `>= acc` is not a span bounded by a runtime value, and `== 4` is not a\nsecond spelling for the literal pattern `4`. Neither is admitted.","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-10-round4/copilot-haiku45/PACKET.md","text":"**To match against a value a name already holds, write `== name`.** A bare name in a pattern\nintroduces a name; `== name` matches the value that name is bound to. **shipped**\n\nSo a head that repeats a bare name — `F(acc, acc)` — is an **error**, not an equality constraint:\nboth are introductions, and the second rebinds what the first bound, which §1 forbids. `F(acc, ==\nacc)` is how you ask for the constraint. This is the whole reason the marker exists; without it the\nlanguage has no way to say *the same value again*.\n\n```csharp\npublic int RunLength(int head, list<int> xs)\n\nRunLength(head, [])                -> 0\nRunLength(head, [== head, ..rest]) -> 1 + RunLength(head, rest)\nRunLength(head, [_, ..rest])       -> 0\n```\n\nIt is the **equality member of the relational family above**, so a reader who has met `>= 4` in a\nhead reads `== acc` on sight. The family divides cleanly — **relational operators take a literal,\n`==` takes a name** — so `>= acc` is not a span bounded by a runtime value, and `== 4` is not a\nsecond spelling for the literal pattern `4`. Neither is admitted.\n\nThe space is not significant: `==acc` and `== acc` are one program. Written with the space, to match\n`>= 4`.\n\nThis is the one capability with no C# equivalent at all — C# patterns cannot match a runtime value,\nand push you to `when v == expected`. Here that workaround is worse than it looks, because it moves\na pattern concern into a guard, and the checker reads `var == literal` but not `var == var`: the\nguard would credit nothing and the arm would subtract nothing from the residual. **A matched name\ncredits nothing to the certain set either.** Its value is unknown at compile time, so it may narrow\nwhat is *possible* and never counts as coverage — a `switch` whose only non-catch-all arm matches a\nname is inexhaustive over the whole subject type.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 3. Exhaustiveness\n\n**A function that does not cover its declared input does not compile.** No opt-out, no flag. The\n**residual is the missing case**, which is why the diagnostic below hands you a clause to paste\nrather than a complaint to interpret.\n\n```csharp\ntype Verdict = :positive | :zero | :negative | :unknown\ntype Reading = (:ok, int) | (:error, atom)\n\npublic Verdict Classify(Reading r)\n\nClassify((:ok, n)) when n > 0 -> :positive\nClassify((:ok, 0))            -> :zero\nClassify((:ok, n))            -> :negative\nClassify((:error, e))         -> :unknown\n```\n\nDelete the third clause and the compiler answers:\n\n```\nerror: Classify is not exhaustive\n  no clause matches:\n    Classify((:ok, n)) when n <= -1 -> ...\n```\n\nThe error is the **missing clause**, not a complaint — the residual is computed exactly and printed\nas a head you can paste in. Where it is wide, the **printed** form stops after three cases and says\nhow many it left; the residual itself is never summarised, and the full one is a query away.\n**shipped**, and the truncation **decided**\n\nA **catch-all is legal only where the residual is open** — over a `term`, or any type with an\nunbounded part. Where the compiler knows the remaining case names, `_` is an error: it would put\nthe language's headline guarantee one character from being switched off invisibly. **shipped** —\nand the diagnostic names the discarded cases as a head to write instead.\n\n---\n\n---\n\n## 5. Control flow\n\n**`switch` is the only branching construct.** There is no `if`, no `else`, no ternary.\n\n<!-- check:\ntype Verdict = :new | :gone | :unknown\nrecord Order { Id: int, Status: atom }\n-->\n```csharp\npublic Verdict Describe(Order o)\n\nDescribe(o) -> o.Status switch {\n    :placed  => :new,\n    :shipped => :gone,\n    _        => :unknown\n}\n```\n\nThe `_` here is legal because `Status` is an `atom` and the atom universe is open, so the residual\ncannot be enumerated — which is the only shape a catch-all is admitted over. Over a *closed*\nresidual, where the compiler knows the missing case by name, §2 makes `_` an error telling you to\nname it — **shipped**, at a switch arm and at a clause head alike.\n\nFor compound conditions, the subject is a **tuple** — which is the clause head's own shape, one","lineStart":193,"lineEnd":292,"totalLines":506,"truncated":true}},"title":"Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-10-round4/copilot-haiku45/PACKET.md","time":{"start":1788998245659,"end":1788998245666}},"metadata":{"anthropic":{"caller":{"type":"direct"}}},"id":"prt_0889ad86d001ofg34IEZcatJaS","sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","messageID":"msg_0889ad386001QJOQ0IBQxDl9Zf"}}
