{"type":"text","timestamp":1788998250815,"sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","part":{"id":"prt_0889ae251001u4JUQ978sUp6s1","messageID":"msg_0889add2d001KYC24hdOpve3OQ","sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","type":"text","text":"I see a key rule: **\"A matched name credits nothing to the certain set either... a `switch` whose only non-catch-all arm matches a name is inexhaustive over the whole subject type.\"**\n\nThis means that a bare name pattern (that introduces a new variable) doesn't count as coverage. Let me check if this is properly handled.\n\nLooking at my current code, I see in the `pattern_covers` and `pattern_could_match_variant` methods, I'm treating bare names as if they cover things, but they shouldn't count as coverage for exhaustiveness. A bare name like `m` or `n` should match anything, but it shouldn't contribute to the \"certain set\" of covered cases.\n\nLet me think about the key issues:\n\n1. **Bare name patterns don't count towards coverage** - This is critical for exhaustiveness. An arm with pattern `n` (a bare name) should match anything but not count for closing the residual.\n\n2. **The catch-all `_` is legal only on open types** - But the current code might be missing this check on closed types.\n\n3. **Relational operators in guards** - Need to ensure proper satisfiability checking.\n\nLet me create a test scenario to understand the expected behavior better. The main issue is likely that bare name patterns are being counted as covering variants when they shouldn't be.","time":{"start":1788998246993,"end":1788998250797}}}
