{"type":"text","timestamp":1788998264777,"sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","part":{"id":"prt_0889b0d28001DNzgqRQswunzmk","messageID":"msg_0889b08420013kZb7BaN5m3Q2U","sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","type":"text","text":"I see the issue now. The spec says catch-all `_` is an error on **closed residuals**. But I need to understand what a \"closed residual\" is. Let me look more carefully:\n\nThe key phrases:\n- \"A catch-all is legal only where the residual is open\"\n- \"Over a `term`, or any type with an unbounded part\"\n- \"Over a closed residual, where the compiler knows the missing case by name\"\n\nThis suggests:\n- Closed types (like unions with specific variants) have closed residuals when all covered cases are enumerated\n- Open types (like `atom`, `int`, `term`) have open residuals\n- Tuples of booleans have closed residuals\n\nSo if we have a closed type like `State = :placed | :shipped | :lost` and our switch covers `:placed` and `:shipped`, the residual is closed and only contains `:lost`. Using `_` instead of `:lost` is an error.\n\nBut there's another scenario: if the subject is a bare name (not a literal), then the switch might not be checking the actual subject. Let me think...\n\nActually, looking at visible case c07-guarded:\n```csharp\nClassify(n) -> n switch {\n    n when n < 5 => :retried,\n    >= 5         => :exhausted\n}\n```\n\nThis reports `rebinding` because the first arm pattern `n` rebinds the parameter `n`. And it still passes because the first arm doesn't actually help with coverage.\n\nBut wait, looking more carefully at what the spec says:\n- \"a `switch` whose only non-catch-all arm matches a name is inexhaustive over the whole subject type\"\n\nThis means if we ONLY have a bare name arm (and maybe a catch-all), it's inexhaustive over a closed type. But if we have bare name arms AND other covering arms, that's fine.\n\nThe key issue I need to identify: **what cases might the current implementation be getting wrong?**\n\nLet me look at what could be wrong by thinking about edge cases:\n\n1. **Catch-all on closed types** - The current code might be incorrectly allowing `_` on closed types\n2. **Bare name patterns not counting as coverage** - The current code might be counting them\n3. **Multiple issues reported** - Maybe I'm reporting too many diagnostics or in the wrong priority\n\nLet me examine the exhaustiveness check more carefully:","time":{"start":1788998257960,"end":1788998264769}}}
