{"type":"text","timestamp":1788998298604,"sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","part":{"id":"prt_0889b930200176ksXUFGpVGQ5g","messageID":"msg_0889b8e61001u247y7mCiJ9PxD","sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","type":"text","text":"Good, the parser is extracting the pattern part correctly. So `>= 5` would be captured as the pattern. Now let me check if the exhaustiveness checking handles this:\n\nLooking at the `arm_covers_variant` method (line 601-611), it only checks if pattern equals variant or if pattern is `_`. It doesn't handle relational patterns like `>= 5`.\n\nFor an `int` type with relational patterns, I need to:\n1. Parse relational patterns like `>= 5`, `< 10`, `>= 4 and <= 7`\n2. Determine which range of integers they cover\n3. Check if all integers are covered\n\nBut wait, the visible test c07-guarded has:\n```csharp\nClassify(n) -> n switch {\n    n when n < 5 => :retried,\n    >= 5         => :exhausted\n}\n```\n\nAnd it reports `rebinding` for the first arm. So the second arm `>= 5` is correctly parsed but might not be correctly checked for coverage.\n\nActually, looking at the output for c07, it ONLY reports `rebinding`, not `switch_inexhaustive`. This suggests that even though the second arm is a relational pattern, it's somehow covering the rest.\n\nOh wait, I think I understand now. The first arm is `n when n < 5` - this means:\n- Pattern `n` (bare name, doesn't count towards coverage)\n- Guard `n < 5` (counts towards coverage)\n\nThe second arm is `>= 5` with no guard:\n- Pattern `>= 5` (relational pattern, counts towards coverage)\n\nSo together they cover all ints: `n < 5` and `>= 5`.\n\nBut the current code reports `rebinding` for the first arm and then stops checking the second arm? Or does it continue?\n\nLet me check the check order. Looking at line 233-240, it checks things in order and returns early for some, but continues for others. Actually, it doesn't return early; it just appends diagnostics.\n\nSo for c07, we should get both `rebinding` AND potentially `switch_inexhaustive`. But the output is only `rebinding`. Let me understand why.\n\nActually, maybe the rule is: if there's a `rebinding` error in an arm, that arm is considered malformed and doesn't contribute to coverage checking?\n\nLet me re-read the spec about rebinding:","time":{"start":1788998292226,"end":1788998298593}}}
