{"type":"text","timestamp":1788998322975,"sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","part":{"id":"prt_0889bfda7001B1Bc8wKuRunwEs","messageID":"msg_0889bf962001touhP6sciUaK2b","sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","type":"text","text":"I see! The subjects are:\n- `o.Status` (field access)\n- `(o, p, r)` (tuple literal)\n- `n`, `b`, `a`, `v`, `e` (parameters)\n- `x switch {...}` (nested switch in guard - this is an error)\n\nSo the language does support field access and tuple construction as switch subjects!\n\nThis means when the subject is `o.Status`, I can't simply look up the type of `o` and assume that's the switch subject type. I need to infer the type of `o.Status`.\n\nBut wait, how can I know the type of a record field? I'd need to parse the record type definition and find the field type. That's complex.\n\nLet me check if maybe the type annotation for the function's parameter helps. If the parameter is `Order o` and I know `Order` is a record with fields including `Status: atom`, then I can infer `o.Status` is `atom`.\n\nBut this requires parsing record definitions, which I haven't seen in the visible test cases. Let me check:","time":{"start":1788998319527,"end":1788998322968}}}
