{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788998395026,"sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","part":{"id":"prt_0889d248b001Mw3KXY4CwK9ESr","messageID":"msg_0889d20d7001sewkFy1wvyoMQt","sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1788998414902,"sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","part":{"id":"prt_0889d2491001mou3KXetBiqwW2","messageID":"msg_0889d20d7001sewkFy1wvyoMQt","sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","type":"text","text":"Excellent! All visible cases still pass. Now let me think about what issues might be in the hidden cases.\n\nThe key insight from my fixes: **removing early returns ensures we report ALL errors, not just the first one per category**.\n\nBut I think there are still more fundamental issues. Let me carefully re-read the spec about key rules:\n\n1. **Bare name patterns don't count towards coverage** - This is important\n2. **Catch-all is illegal on closed residuals** - But there's no diagnostic tag for this\n3. **Relational patterns** - Need proper handling\n\nLet me think about the catch-all issue more carefully. Looking at c04-unreachable:\n```csharp\nDescribe(s) -> s switch {\n    :placed  => :new,\n    :shipped => :gone,\n    :placed  => :again\n}\n```\n\nThis correctly reports `unreachable_arm` for the third arm.\n\nBut what if we had:\n```csharp\nDescribe(s) -> s switch {\n    :placed  => :new,\n    :shipped => :gone,\n    _        => :unknown\n}\n```\n\nAccording to the spec, this should be an error because `_` is not allowed on a closed residual (closed type `State`). But there's no diagnostic tag for this!\n\nUnless... maybe the `_` itself is not considered coverage for a closed type, so it would be unreachable? Let me think...\n\nIf `_` doesn't count as coverage for closed types, then it's an unreachable arm. But that doesn't match the semantic meaning.\n\nActually, re-reading the spec more carefully: \"Where the compiler knows the remaining case names, `_` is an error telling you to name it\"\n\nThis suggests that when the residual is closed (known case names), using `_` is an error. But the error might be reported as exhaustiveness failure, not as unreachable_arm.\n\nWait, but if all closed cases are covered, then the switch IS exhaustive, so there's no exhaustiveness error. The `_` is just redundant/illegal.\n\nActually, maybe the issue is that I'm not checking for illegal catch-all at all! Let me look for where this should be reported.\n\nLooking at the diagnostics, there's no tag for \"illegal_catchall\". So either:\n1. It's part of one of the other checks\n2. It's not tested in the visible cases but might be in hidden cases\n3. The spec description is just explaining the rule, but the checker doesn't need to enforce it\n\nLet me re-read the spec to see if catch-all on closed types should produce an error:\n\n\"Over a *closed* residual, where the compiler knows the missing case by name, §2 makes `_` an error telling you to name it\"\n\nSo it IS an error. But what error? Maybe it's reported as part of the exhaustiveness check?\n\nActually, let me think about this differently. If we have:\n```\ntype State = :placed | :shipped | :lost\nDescribe(s) -> s switch {\n    :placed => :new,\n    _ => :unknown\n}\n```\n\nIs this:\n- Inexhaustive (missing `:shipped` and `:lost`)?\n- Or exhaustive (because `_` covers everything)?\n\nAccording to the spec, catch-all is illegal on closed residuals. So maybe the compiler should report it as inexhaustive because the `_` is not allowed?\n\nActually, rereading again: \"A catch-all is legal only where the residual is open... Where the compiler knows the remaining case names, `_` is an error\"\n\nSo the rule is: when the residual is closed (remaining cases are known), `_` is illegal.\n\nBut I think the way to handle this is: when checking exhaustiveness on a closed type, if I find a catch-all, I should treat it as if it's not there for coverage purposes! That way:\n- If all explicit patterns cover the type, the switch is exhaustive (and the catch-all is redundant but present)\n- If not all explicit patterns cover the type, the switch is inexhaustive\n\nBut that doesn't quite make sense either, because `_` DOES match all values.\n\nLet me look at c02 (catch-all on open type):\n```csharp\nDescribe(s) -> s switch {\n    :placed  => :new,\n    :shipped => :gone,\n    _        => :unknown\n}\n```\n\nThis correctly produces NO diagnostics. The type is `atom` (open), so catch-all is allowed.\n\nNow, if the type were closed:\n```csharp\ntype State = :placed | :shipped | :lost\nDescribe(s) -> s switch {\n    :placed  => :new,\n    :shipped => :gone,\n    _        => :unknown\n}\n```\n\nAccording to the spec, this is an error. But what error code? There's no specific code for \"illegal catch-all\".\n\nOhh, I think I understand now! Maybe the catch-all should be treated as vacuous? No, that doesn't make sense.\n\nActually, maybe it should cause `switch_inexhaustive` to be reported, because we REQUIRE naming the specific case instead of using `_`?\n\nLet me implement that logic: on a closed type, if there's a catch-all, report inexhaustiveness. The residual message would be the uncovered cases (if any) or just flag that catch-all isn't allowed.\n\nBut wait, if all cases are explicitly covered, then there IS no residual, so it wouldn't be inexhaustive.\n\nHmm, let me look at this from another angle. Maybe the spec is just saying what the RULE is, but the checker doesn't explicitly enforce it if it doesn't result in an error. The key point is that catch-all can only appear on open residuals. If it appears on a closed residual, either:\n1. All cases are covered explicitly (so `_` is redundant but not an error because each case was still named)\n2. Some cases are missing (so `_` is needed to cover them, but that's illegal for closed types, so... it's an error?)\n\nI think the issue is: on a closed residual, if you use `_`, you MUST ALSO have all explicit cases covered. Otherwise it's an error.\n\nActually, I think the simpler interpretation is: on a closed type, ANY use of `_` is an error. It's not allowed.\n\nSo my check should be: if `param_type` is a closed type and there's a `_` arm, report something. But what?\n\nLooking at the spec again: \"Where the compiler knows the remaining case names, `_` is an error telling you to name it — **shipped** — and the diagnostic names the discarded cases as a head to write instead.\"\n\nSo the diagnostic would tell you to name the specific case instead of using `_`. This sounds like it might be part of the exhaustiveness check!\n\nLet me revise my understanding: maybe when there's a `_` on a closed type, and not all cases are explicitly covered, it should report `switch_inexhaustive` with the message about naming the specific cases.\n\nAnd if a `_` on a closed type covers all cases explicitly covered + the missing ones, then... that's not exhaustive in the sense the spec wants?\n\nActually, I think I'm overcomplicating this. Let me just implement: on a closed type, if there's a catch-all and not all cases are explicitly matched by non-catch-all arms, report inexhaustiveness.\n\nLet me modify the exhaustiveness check:","time":{"start":1788998395025,"end":1788998414890}}}
