{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788998432779,"sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","part":{"id":"prt_0889db7fc001ZhilXvdQjmTl4H","messageID":"msg_0889db335001i5GU8tiIZ9vAg4","sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1788998441059,"sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","part":{"id":"prt_0889db808001u9w7gjW9gwwxUo","messageID":"msg_0889db335001i5GU8tiIZ9vAg4","sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","type":"text","text":"Interesting: \"A bare `n` as an arm pattern is rejected with no guard present at all; `m when m < 5` and a plain `< 5` are both accepted.\"\n\nSo:\n- `n` (bare name, no guard) - REJECTED if n is already bound\n- `m when m < 5` (bare name with guard, m is fresh) - ACCEPTED\n- `< 5` (relational pattern, no name) - ACCEPTED\n\nThis is consistent with my current checks.\n\nBut wait, looking at the example again:\n```\nClassify(n) -> n switch {\n    m when m < 5 => :retried,\n    >= 5         => :exhausted\n}\n```\n\nBoth arms are accepted. `m when m < 5` introduces a fresh name `m`, and `>= 5` is a relational pattern. So both are valid.\n\nNow, the first arm has pattern `m` (bare name, fresh) - it matches any `int`. The second arm has pattern `>= 5` - it matches ints >= 5. Are these exhaustive over `int`?\n\nWell, `int` is infinite, so it's an open type. My check for exhaustiveness on open non-tuple types just returns without checking, assuming they're OK. So no exhaustiveness error.\n\nBut is there actually an exhaustiveness issue? Let me think:\n- `m when m < 5` matches ints < 5\n- `>= 5` matches ints >= 5\n\nTogether they cover all ints! So it's exhaustive.\n\nOK so that's correct.\n\nNow, let me think about what could be failing. Maybe the issue is with how I'm handling guards?\n\nActually, wait. Looking at my `pattern_covers` function:\n\n```python\ndef pattern_covers(self, p1: str, p2: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Does pattern p1 cover pattern p2?\"\"\"\n    p1 = p1.strip()\n    p2 = p2.strip()\n    \n    if p1 == '_':\n        return True\n    \n    if p1 == p2:\n        return True\n    \n    return False\n```\n\nThis only checks if p1 equals p2 or p1 is `_`. But what if p1 is a bare name like `m` and p2 is a literal like `:placed`? They're not equal, so it returns `False`. But doesn't a bare name match anything?\n\nAccording to the spec, a bare name DOES match anything (in terms of pattern matching). So `m` covers ANY pattern! This includes `_` and any literal.\n\nBut wait, the spec also says bare names don't count as coverage. So there's a distinction:\n- Pattern matching: bare names match anything\n- Coverage: bare names don't count as coverage for exhaustiveness\n\nSo when checking if an earlier arm covers a later arm for `unreachable_arm`, I should use pattern matching logic. But when checking exhaustiveness, I should use coverage logic.\n\nCurrently, I'm using `pattern_covers` for the unreachable arm check, which doesn't count bare names as coverage. But it should!\n\nLet me fix this:","time":{"start":1788998432777,"end":1788998441053}}}
