{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788998456752,"sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","part":{"id":"prt_0889e15aa001l1kSZzBnEgjSiy","messageID":"msg_0889e1126001IvTF27nmoVhAP6","sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1788998466527,"sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","part":{"id":"prt_0889e15ad001nQNXzn9Av8nR1Y","messageID":"msg_0889e1126001IvTF27nmoVhAP6","sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","type":"text","text":"Oh no! Now c07 and c09 are reporting extra `unreachable_arm` errors that weren't there before. This is because I made bare names cover everything.\n\nBut wait, that's actually correct behavior according to the spec! If the first arm has a bare name pattern, it matches everything, so all subsequent arms are unreachable.\n\nLet me check c07 again:\n\n```\nClassify(n) -> n switch {\n    n when n < 5 => :retried,\n    >= 5         => :exhausted\n}\n```\n\nThe first arm is `n` (bare name) with guard `n < 5`. The second arm is `>= 5`.\n\nNow, does the first arm with guard `n < 5` match everything? No! It only matches when the guard is true. So the second arm is not unreachable.\n\nAh, I see the issue. When checking unreachability, I need to consider the guard! An arm with an unsatisfiable guard covers nothing. But an arm with a guard that's not unsatisfiable might cover something but not everything.\n\nSo the logic should be:\n- A bare name pattern matches everything\n- UNLESS it has a guard that restricts it\n- In that case, the pattern matches values where the guard is true\n\nFor unreachability, arm2 is unreachable by arm1 if:\n- arm1's pattern matches arm2's pattern (considering guards)\n\nSo I need to check guards when determining coverage.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The spec says \"An arm every earlier arm already covers is dead.\"\n\nWhat does \"covers\" mean here? I think it means \"all values that reach arm2 also reached arm1\".\n\nSo if arm1 has pattern `n when n < 5`, it matches:\n- All values where n < 5 is true\n\nAnd arm2 has pattern `>= 5`, it matches:\n- All values >= 5\n\nThese don't overlap! So arm2 is not unreachable.\n\nBut in c09:\n```\npublic atom Check(int n)\nCheck(n) -> n switch {\n    m when Big(m) => Tag(m),\n    _             => :small\n}\n```\n\nArm1 is `m when Big(m)`. The guard `Big(m)` depends on calling a function Big, which I can't statically determine. So I don't know what values it covers.\n\nArm2 is `_` (catch-all). Does arm1 cover everything? I can't tell, because the guard is non-deterministic to my checker.\n\nAccording to the spec: \"A guard the checker **cannot read** — one comparing two variables, say — is not this, and is never reported: an arm is judged on its pattern alone there, rather than the compiler announcing its own ignorance as your mistake.\"\n\nSo when I can't read the guard (like a function call), I should judge the arm on its pattern alone! That means:\n- Pattern `m` (bare name) matches everything\n- Guard `Big(m)` - I can't evaluate this, so I ignore it for coverage purposes\n- Therefore, arm1 covers everything\n- And arm2 `_` is unreachable\n\nSo c09 reporting `unreachable_arm` is correct! But the original test didn't report it...\n\nLet me check what the original test output was:","time":{"start":1788998456749,"end":1788998466513}}}
