{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788998539589,"sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","part":{"id":"prt_0889f593f001U4eZxOq0UT4udD","messageID":"msg_0889f554f0010gSKBFx9Yqnenq","sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1788998541775,"sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","part":{"id":"prt_0889f59430015Jzlw3DyodElw4","messageID":"msg_0889f554f0010gSKBFx9Yqnenq","sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","type":"text","text":"Hmm, this produces no output too. But according to my code, when there's a catch-all on a closed type, I return without checking. So I'm not reporting that `:lost` is missing.\n\nIs this correct? According to the spec: \"A catch-all is legal only where the residual is open.\"\n\nSo using `_` on a closed type (when not all cases are covered) is illegal. But I'm not reporting it!\n\nLet me reconsider. Maybe the intention is that when there's a catch-all, we don't report inexhaustiveness even on closed types? That would be weird though.\n\nOr maybe the catch-all should be treated as covering the missing cases, so it's exhaustive?\n\nActually, let me re-read the spec more carefully about this:","time":{"start":1788998539587,"end":1788998541764}}}
