{"type":"text","timestamp":1788998592727,"sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","part":{"id":"prt_088a00e5f00153WuRIlg3QAUiO","messageID":"msg_088a008ad001pMlVemBTWl1heS","sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","type":"text","text":"So for open types (non-closed), I only check tuple exhaustiveness. For other open types like `int`, `atom`, `term`, I don't check anything.\n\nThis makes sense because open types are infinite, so they're never exhaustive unless there's a catch-all.\n\nBut wait, if we have:\n```\npublic int Grade(int n)\nGrade(n) -> n switch {\n    m when m < 5 => :low,\n    m when m >= 5 and m < 10 => :mid,\n    m when m >= 10 => :high\n}\n```\n\nIs this exhaustive? The guards together cover all integers. But my code doesn't check this.\n\nHowever, this might not be a problem because:\n1. The visible test c07 doesn't report an exhaustiveness error for a similar case\n2. The spec might not require exhaustiveness checking for open types with guards\n\nLet me check c07 again:\n\n```\nClassify(n) -> n switch {\n    n when n < 5 => :retried,\n    >= 5         => :exhausted\n}\n```\n\nThis doesn't produce an exhaustiveness error in the output. So either:\n1. It's exhaustive and my code is correct\n2. It's not exhaustive but the spec doesn't require reporting it\n3. There's a different error that masks the exhaustiveness error\n\nThe output for c07 is just `rebinding`, so there's definitely no exhaustiveness error being reported.\n\nActually, the first arm has pattern `n` (rebinding error), but that doesn't stop the checker from continuing. So the question is: if we ignore the rebinding error and just look at coverage, is the switch exhaustive?\n\n- Arm 1: pattern `n`, guard `n < 5` - covers ints < 5 (but bare name doesn't count for coverage)\n- Arm 2: pattern `>= 5` - covers ints >= 5 (but relational pattern doesn't match a literal, so doesn't count for coverage)\n\nSo neither arm is counted as coverage, and the switch should be inexhaustive! But it's not reported.\n\nHmm, maybe the spec doesn't require exhaustiveness checking for `int` type (open type)?\n\nOr maybe my code is correct in not reporting it, because the arms DO collectively cover all integers at runtime, even if the static coverage analysis says otherwise.\n\nLet me check if there's a note in the spec about this:","time":{"start":1788998585951,"end":1788998592714}}}
