{"type":"text","timestamp":1788998595739,"sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","part":{"id":"prt_088a02f22001zr5qF0fV2cJBNk","messageID":"msg_088a02a8c001coNhEoDScQtFkT","sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","type":"text","text":"OK so the spec says \"open types\" are those where the residual is open. These include `atom`, `int`, `term`, and types with unbounded parts like `list<int>`.\n\nAnd it says: \"A catch-all is legal only where the residual is open.\"\n\nThis implies that for open types, you DON'T need to cover all cases - a catch-all is legal. But do you still need to check exhaustiveness? Let me re-read:","time":{"start":1788998594338,"end":1788998595729}}}
