{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788998641857,"sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","part":{"id":"prt_088a0e8bf001fMJ3qg4MKu4Lrb","messageID":"msg_088a0e407001Kol1y2YTVd6oU2","sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1788998645140,"sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","part":{"id":"prt_088a0e8c0001NunV0y1lxWSh2U","messageID":"msg_088a0e407001Kol1y2YTVd6oU2","sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","type":"text","text":"Perfect! All still pass. Now let me think about other issues.\n\nActually, I'm wondering if maybe the issue is with how I'm handling the subject of the switch. Let me check c09 more carefully:\n\nThe function Check takes `int n`. It calls `Check(n) -> n switch {...}`. So the subject is `n`, which is of type `int`.\n\nBut what if the subject is an expression like `o.Status`? Then I need to infer the type of the expression, which requires parsing the record type.\n\nLooking at my code:\n\n```python\nsubject_match = re.search(r'(\\w+)\\s+switch\\s*\\{', switch_text)\nsubject = subject_match.group(1) if subject_match else None\n```\n\nThis only extracts the first word before `switch`. So for `o.Status switch`, it would extract `o`, not `o.Status`.\n\nIs this a problem? Let me think about when this is used. The `subject` field is set in SwitchArm, but I don't see it being used anywhere in the checks! Let me search:","time":{"start":1788998641856,"end":1788998645134}}}
