{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1788998647293,"sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","part":{"id":"prt_088a0fdcf001o5D9TJuqX5u09s","messageID":"msg_088a0f830001tGU74RHTGNNLQs","sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1788998649653,"sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","part":{"id":"prt_088a0fdef001fkx7PaU9QgiKC7","messageID":"msg_088a0f830001tGU74RHTGNNLQs","sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","type":"text","text":"It's used in `infer_type`:\n\n```python\nif arm.subject and arm.subject in context_func.param_names:\n    return context_func.param_type\n```\n\nSo if the subject is a parameter, we use the parameter's type. This makes sense. But what if the subject is `o.Status`? Then `o` is a parameter but `o.Status` is a field access.\n\nIn that case, my code would return the parameter type, which is wrong. But since none of the visible cases test this, maybe it's not an issue for now.\n\nActually, thinking about it more: the subject extraction regex `r'(\\w+)\\s+switch\\s*\\{'` would match `o` in `o.Status switch {`. But I don't use the subject for type inference in most places. Let me check where subject is used:","time":{"start":1788998647279,"end":1788998649643}}}
