{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788998654763,"sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"toolu_01ASwEm44axfAGxuUke7sZJb","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-10-round4/copilot-haiku45/PACKET.md","limit":50,"offset":143},"output":"<path>/Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-10-round4/copilot-haiku45/PACKET.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n143: -->\n144: ```csharp\n145: Classify(>= 4 and <= 7) -> :reserved\n146: Classify(<= -1)         -> :negative\n147: Classify(>= 0 and <= 3) -> :low\n148: Classify(>= 8)          -> :high\n149: ```\n150: \n151: Those four clauses are **exhaustive over `int`** with no catch-all, which is the property worth\n152: looking at: a span is a set the checker subtracts, not a test it takes on trust. It goes where a\n153: whole argument goes — inside a record pattern, a tuple or a list, write the comparison as a guard.\n154: \n155: The rule this produced, which governs future borrowings: **borrow the construct, or don't borrow\n156: the glyph.** Where C# has the symbol but not the construct, taking the symbol buys no familiarity\n157: and costs a false friend.\n158: \n159: **A list pattern is a prefix, and a rest marker is optional.** `[a, b]` is exactly two; `[a, b, ..]`\n160: is two or more and discards the tail; `[a, b, ..t]` is two or more and binds it. The marker is a\n161: marker and not a pattern — `..` or `..name`, nothing else — so a list pattern says how long the list\n162: is and what is in the positions it names, and nothing about the rest. **shipped**\n163: \n164: `[a, b]` means exactly two in Erlang, Elixir, C# and Gleam alike. That is the one place this\n165: language's two reference families agree, so refusing it was the divergence rather than admitting it\n166: — and the refusal used to advise `[a, b, ..t]`, which means something else.\n167: \n168: <!-- check:\n169: public atom Dispatch(list<string> path)\n170: -->\n171: ```csharp\n172: Dispatch([\"orders\"])     -> :index\n173: Dispatch([\"orders\", id]) -> :show\n174: Dispatch(_)              -> :not_found\n175: ```\n176: \n177: That is how a route table distinguishes `/orders` from `/orders/42` without a length guard, and\n178: `/orders/42/lines` reaches the catch-all rather than being swallowed by the second clause.\n179: \n180: **The checker sees the length, and it does so without ever measuring one.** A non-empty list is a\n181: product of an element and a tail, subtracted by the same rule that already subtracts tuples exactly,\n182: so length falls out of the recursion rather than being carried beside it. The residual is then a\n183: clause you can paste: `[]` beside `[a, b, ..]` leaves `[int]` — exactly-one — where a language with\n184: an O(1) length would say `{ Length: 1 }` and this one has no `length` to say it with. Depth is\n185: bounded by the longest prefix any clause writes, per nesting level, which is what makes the\n186: recursion terminate. **shipped**\n187: \n188: A consequence worth stating: a closed residual over a list forbids a catch-all exactly as any other\n189: closed residual does, so a `list<bool>` missing its two length-one cases is an error naming\n190: `[true]` and `[false]` rather than a `_`. That bites only where the element type is closed — over\n191: `list<int>` the element is unbounded, the residual stays open, and `_` remains legal.\n192: \n\n(Showing lines 143-192 of 506. Use offset=193 to continue.)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"-->\n```csharp\nClassify(>= 4 and <= 7) -> :reserved\nClassify(<= -1)         -> :negative\nClassify(>= 0 and <= 3) -> :low\nClassify(>= 8)          -> :high\n```\n\nThose four clauses are **exhaustive over `int`** with no catch-all, which is the property worth\nlooking at: a span is a set the checker subtracts, not a test it takes on trust. It goes where a\nwhole argument goes — inside a record pattern, a tuple or a list, write the comparison as a guard.\n\nThe rule this produced, which governs future borrowings: **borrow the construct, or don't borrow\nthe glyph.** Where C# has the symbol but not the construct, taking the symbol buys no familiarity\nand costs a false friend.\n\n**A list pattern is a prefix, and a rest marker is optional.** `[a, b]` is exactly two; `[a, b, ..]`\nis two or more and discards the tail; `[a, b, ..t]` is two or more and binds it. The marker is a\nmarker and not a pattern — `..` or `..name`, nothing else — so a list pattern says how long the list\nis and what is in the positions it names, and nothing about the rest. **shipped**","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-10-round4/copilot-haiku45/PACKET.md","text":"-->\n```csharp\nClassify(>= 4 and <= 7) -> :reserved\nClassify(<= -1)         -> :negative\nClassify(>= 0 and <= 3) -> :low\nClassify(>= 8)          -> :high\n```\n\nThose four clauses are **exhaustive over `int`** with no catch-all, which is the property worth\nlooking at: a span is a set the checker subtracts, not a test it takes on trust. It goes where a\nwhole argument goes — inside a record pattern, a tuple or a list, write the comparison as a guard.\n\nThe rule this produced, which governs future borrowings: **borrow the construct, or don't borrow\nthe glyph.** Where C# has the symbol but not the construct, taking the symbol buys no familiarity\nand costs a false friend.\n\n**A list pattern is a prefix, and a rest marker is optional.** `[a, b]` is exactly two; `[a, b, ..]`\nis two or more and discards the tail; `[a, b, ..t]` is two or more and binds it. The marker is a\nmarker and not a pattern — `..` or `..name`, nothing else — so a list pattern says how long the list\nis and what is in the positions it names, and nothing about the rest. **shipped**\n\n`[a, b]` means exactly two in Erlang, Elixir, C# and Gleam alike. That is the one place this\nlanguage's two reference families agree, so refusing it was the divergence rather than admitting it\n— and the refusal used to advise `[a, b, ..t]`, which means something else.\n\n<!-- check:\npublic atom Dispatch(list<string> path)\n-->\n```csharp\nDispatch([\"orders\"])     -> :index\nDispatch([\"orders\", id]) -> :show\nDispatch(_)              -> :not_found\n```\n\nThat is how a route table distinguishes `/orders` from `/orders/42` without a length guard, and\n`/orders/42/lines` reaches the catch-all rather than being swallowed by the second clause.\n\n**The checker sees the length, and it does so without ever measuring one.** A non-empty list is a\nproduct of an element and a tail, subtracted by the same rule that already subtracts tuples exactly,\nso length falls out of the recursion rather than being carried beside it. The residual is then a\nclause you can paste: `[]` beside `[a, b, ..]` leaves `[int]` — exactly-one — where a language with\nan O(1) length would say `{ Length: 1 }` and this one has no `length` to say it with. Depth is\nbounded by the longest prefix any clause writes, per nesting level, which is what makes the\nrecursion terminate. **shipped**\n\nA consequence worth stating: a closed residual over a list forbids a catch-all exactly as any other\nclosed residual does, so a `list<bool>` missing its two length-one cases is an error naming\n`[true]` and `[false]` rather than a `_`. That bites only where the element type is closed — over\n`list<int>` the element is unbounded, the residual stays open, and `_` remains legal.\n","lineStart":143,"lineEnd":192,"totalLines":506,"truncated":true}},"title":"Volumes/Personal/Users/davidwilliams/.ringer/audition-2026-09-10-round4/copilot-haiku45/PACKET.md","time":{"start":1788998654733,"end":1788998654745}},"metadata":{"anthropic":{"caller":{"type":"direct"}}},"id":"prt_088a1189d001cbsnXiKchAPslG","sessionID":"ses_f7765817fffe5KXGQdzWpZbY01","messageID":"msg_088a113d8001y4IKhJWAB6SrR8"}}
